window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-04"]=[{"id":"q2-61","q":"2:61","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:61 · Rejecting the allotted provision","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 113–115","cairo":"I:113–115","source_page_first":113,"source_page_last":115,"ar":"وَإِذْ قُلْتُمْ يَا مُوسى لَنْ نَصْبِرَ عَلى طَعامٍ واحِدٍ فَادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُخْرِجْ لَنا مِمَّا تُنْبِتُ الْأَرْضُ مِنْ بَقْلِها وَقِثَّائِها وَفُومِها وَعَدَسِها وَبَصَلِها قالَ أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنى بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ اهْبِطُوا مِصْراً فَإِنَّ لَكُمْ ما سَأَلْتُمْ وَضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الذِّلَّةُ وَالْمَسْكَنَةُ وَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ كانُوا يَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَيَقْتُلُونَ النَّبِيِّينَ بِغَيْرِ الْحَقِّ ذلِكَ بِما عَصَوْا وَكانُوا يَعْتَدُونَ (61)\nقلت: المراد بالطعام الواحد: هو المنّ والسلوى. ووحَّده لأنه لا يختلف ولا يتبدل، كقولهم: طعام مائدة الأمير واحد، والبقل: جميع الخضر، كالنجم والكرنب والكراث وغير ذلك. والقثاء: جمع قثاءة، وهي الخيار والفقوس والبطيخ وغير ذلك من الفواكه التي تستنبت، والفوم قيل: الحنطة، والأصح أنه الثوم. قال الشاعر:\nوأنتم أُناسٌ لِئامُ الأُصولِ ... طعامُكُم الفومُ والحَوقَلُ\nأراد: الثوم والبصل. والعرب تعاقب بين الفاء والثاء فتقول: معافير ومعاثير، وتقول للقبر: جدث وجدف.\nوالعدس: معلوم، روى علي- كرم الله وجهه- عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم أنه قال: «عليكم بالعدس، فإنه مبارك مقدس، وإنه يرقق القلب، ويكثر الدمعة، وإنه بارك فيه سبعون نبيا، آخرهم عيسى بن مريم»(1).\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا أيضاً حين قُلْتُمْ يَا مُوسى لَنْ نَصْبِرَ عَلى طَعامٍ واحِدٍ حين مللتم من العسل واللحم، وملتم إلى عَكَرِكُمْ السوء، أي: مألوفكم وشهواتكم السيئة، لأنهم كانوا فلاحين، فقلتم: فَادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُخْرِجْ لَنا مِمَّا تُنْبِتُ الْأَرْضُ، أي: من جنس ما ينبت الله فيها من البقل والقثاء والعدس والفوم والبصل، قال موسى عليه السلام: أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنى وأخس من الثوم والبصل وغيرهما، بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ من اللحم والعسل، اهْبِطُوا إلى مصر من الأمصار، تجدوا ما تشتهون، إذ لا يوجد ذلك إلا في القرى والأمصار، أو اهْبِطُوا مِصْراً التي كنتم فيها أذلاء مستعبدين، تجدوا حظوظكم وشهواتكم لأن الحظوظ والشهوات منوطة بالذل والهوان، وَضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الذِّلَّةُ وَالْمَسْكَنَةُ، أي: ألزموها لزوم الدرهم المضروب لضربه ونقشه، فالذلة: ضرب الجزية، والمسكنة: فقر النفس وإن كان موسراً.\nوإنما ضُربت عليهم الذلة والمسكنة لأنهم لم يرضوا بتدبير الحق، ولم يقنعوا برزقه، فكل مَن لم يقنع بقسمته وسلم من اتحاد رزقه، خيف عليه من ضرب الذل والمسكنة، وانقلبوا أيضاً بِغَضَبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ حيث نقضوا العهود، وتعدوا الحدود، فكفروا وطغوا وقتلوا الأنبياء بغير حق، وسبب ذلك: تمردهم في العصيان، فإن المعاصي تجر بعضها إلى البعض حتى تنتهي إلى الكفر، والعياذ بالله من سخطه وغضبه.\nالإشارة: كل مَن لم يقنع بالقسمة الأزلية، ولم يقم حيث أقامته القدرة الإلهية، بل جنح إلى حظوظه وهواه، وحرص على تحصيل أغراضه ومناه، قيل له: أتستبدل تدبيرك- الذي هو أدنى- بتدبير الحق- الذي هو خير-؟\nأتترك تدبير الحكيم العليم، الرؤوف الرحيم، إلى تدبير عقلك الضعيف الجاهل الخسيس اللئيم؟! فعسى أن تدبر شيئاً يكون لك فإذا هو عليك. وعسى أن تأتيك المسار من حيث تعتقد المضار، وتأتيك المضار من حيث ترتجي المسار.\nولله درّ القائل:\nوكَم رُمْتُ أَمْراً خِرْتَ لي في انْصِرَافِهِ، ... فَلاَ زلْتَ لي مِني أبَرَّ وأَرْحَمَا\nعَزَمْتُ عَلَى ألاّ أُحِسَّ بِخَاطِرٍ ... عَلى القلْبِ إلاَّ كُنْتَ أَنْتَ المُقَدَّمَا\nوألا تَرَانِي عِنْدَ مَا قَدْ نَهيْتَني ... لِكَونَك(1) في قَلْبِي كَبيراً معظّما\nيا مَن لم يقنع بتدبير مولاه، ومال إلى نيل حظه وهواه، اهبط إلى أرض الحظوظ والشهوات تجد فيها ما ألفته نفسك من عوائدك السيئات. يا مَن أخلدت نفسه إلى الهوى ومتابعة الشيطان، كيف تستبدل العز الدائم بالذل والهوان؟! وأنشدوا:\nلاَ تتبع النفس في هواها ... إنَّ اتِّبَاعَ الْهَوَى هَوَانُ(1)\nقال في التنوير: (فائدة) اعلم إن بني إسرائيل لمَّا دخلوا التيه، ورُزقوا المنّ والسلوى، واختار الله لهم ذلك رزقاً، رزقهم إياه، يبرز من عين المنة، من غير تعب منهم ولا نصب، فرجعت نفوسهم الكثيفة لوجود، العادة، والغيبة عن شهود تدبير الله، إلى طلب ما كانوا يعتادونه، فقالوا: فَادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُخْرِجْ لَنا مِمَّا تُنْبِتُ الْأَرْضُ الآية. قالَ أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنى بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ. اهْبِطُوا مِصْراً فَإِنَّ لَكُمْ ما سَأَلْتُمْ. وَضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الذِّلَّةُ وَالْمَسْكَنَةُ وَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ، وذلك لأنهم تركوا ما اختار الله لهم، مائلين لما اختاروا لأنفسهم. فقيل لهم عن طريق التوبيخ: أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنى بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ؟ فظاهر التفسير: أتستبدلون الفوم والعدس والبصل بالمنّ والسلوى؟ وليس النوعان سواء في اللذة ولا فى سقوط المشقة وسر الاعتبار، أتستبدلون مرادكم لأنفسكم بمراد الله تعالى لكم؟ أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنى وهو ما أردتموه، بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ، وهو ما أراده الله لكم؟ اهْبِطُوا مِصْراً فإن ما اشتهيتموه لا يليق إلا أن يكون في الأمصار، وفي سر الخطاب: اهبطوا عن سماء التفويض وحسن التدبير منا لكم، إلى أرض التدبير والاختيار منكم لأنفسكم، موصوفين بالذل والمسكنة لاختياركم مع اختيار الله، وتدبيركم لأنفسكم مع تدبير الله. هـ المراد منه.\nولما ذَكَّرَهُمْ الحق تعالى بالنعمة، ووبَّخَهم على ارتكاب الآثام، رغّبهم فى الإسلام، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قُلْتُمْ يَا مُوسى لَنْ نَصْبِرَ عَلى طَعامٍ واحِدٍ فَادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُخْرِجْ لَنا مِمَّا تُنْبِتُ الْأَرْضُ مِنْ بَقْلِها وَقِثَّائِها وَفُومِها وَعَدَسِها وَبَصَلِها قالَ أَتَسْتَبْدِلُونَ الَّذِي هُوَ أَدْنى بِالَّذِي هُوَ خَيْرٌ اهْبِطُوا مِصْراً فَإِنَّ لَكُمْ ما سَأَلْتُمْ وَضُرِبَتْ عَلَيْهِمُ الذِّلَّةُ وَالْمَسْكَنَةُ وَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ ذلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ كانُوا يَكْفُرُونَ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَيَقْتُلُونَ النَّبِيِّينَ بِغَيْرِ الْحَقِّ ذلِكَ بِما عَصَوْا وَكانُوا يَعْتَدُونَ (61)","quran_en":"61. When you said, “Moses, we cannot endure one kind of food. Pray to your Lord for us to bring forth some of what the earth grows: its herbs, cucumbers, garlic, lentils and onions.” He said, “Would you exchange what is better for what is inferior? Go down to a town; there you shall have what you asked for.” Abasement and poverty were imposed upon them, and they incurred wrath from God. That was because they denied God’s signs and killed the prophets without right; that was because they disobeyed and habitually transgressed.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The foods named in the verse","html":"<p>I say: “one kind of food” means manna and quails. It is called one because it did not vary or change, as when one says that a prince’s table always offers one meal. Herbs (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البقل<\/bdi>) embraces all greens: low-growing herbs, cabbage, leeks and others. <i>Qiththāʾ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القثّاء<\/bdi>), plural of <i>qiththāʾa<\/i>, includes cucumbers, snake-cucumbers, melons and other cultivated fruits. <i>Fūm<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفوم<\/bdi>) is said to be wheat; more correctly, it is garlic. The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">You are a people of base origins:<br>your food is garlic and onions.<\/blockquote><p>He means garlic by <i>fūm<\/i> and onions by <i>ḥawqal<\/i>. The Arabs interchange <i>fāʾ<\/i> and <i>thāʾ<\/i>: they say <i>maʿāfīr<\/i> and <i>maʿāthīr<\/i>, and call a grave <i>jadath<\/i> or <i>jadaf<\/i>.<\/p><p>Lentils are well known. ʿAlī—God ennoble his face—relates from the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace: “Take lentils, for they are blessed and holy; they soften the heart and increase tears. Seventy prophets blessed them, the last being Jesus son of Mary.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-51\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B047]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “one kind of food” means manna and quails. It is called one because it did not vary or change, as when one says that a prince’s table always offers one meal. Herbs ( البقل ) embraces all greens: low-growing herbs, cabbage, leeks and others. Qiththāʾ ( القثّاء ), plural of qiththāʾa , includes cucumbers, snake-cucumbers, melons and other cultivated fruits. Fūm ( الفوم ) is said to be wheat; more correctly, it is garlic. The poet says: You are a people of base origins: your food is garlic and onions. He means garlic by fūm and onions by ḥawqal . The Arabs interchange fāʾ and thāʾ : they say maʿāfīr and maʿāthīr , and call a grave jadath or jadaf . Lentils are well known. ʿAlī—God ennoble his face—relates from the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace: “Take lentils, for they are blessed and holy; they soften the heart and increase tears. Seventy prophets blessed them, the last being Jesus son of Mary.” [B047]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Discontent and the preference for familiar habits","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when you said, “Moses, we cannot endure one food,” tiring of honey and meat and inclining towards your own bad sediment—that is, your familiar practices and bad desires, since they had been agricultural people. You said: ask your Lord to bring forth some of what He grows on earth: greens, cucumbers, lentils, garlic and onions. Moses asked: do you exchange the better—meat and honey—for the lower and baser—garlic, onions and the rest? Go down to any town and you will find your cravings, for these foods occur in villages and towns. Alternatively: return to Egypt, where you were humiliated and enslaved, and find your egoic shares and desires, for such shares and desires are bound to humiliation and abasement.<\/p><p>Abasement and poverty were imposed upon them, clinging as a struck coin retains its impression and engraving. Abasement is the imposition of the tribute-tax (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الجزية<\/bdi>); poverty is poverty of the soul, even when materially wealthy. These were imposed because they were not content with the Real’s ordering or satisfied with His provision. Whoever is discontent with his allotted share and tires of the sameness of his sustenance [Translator’s note: Cairo I:114 prints <bdi dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وسلم<\/bdi>, “and was free/safe,” apparently a corruption of <bdi dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وسئم<\/bdi>, “and grew tired.” The rendering follows the latter conjecture, supported by the passage’s opening reference to weariness with manna and quails.] is in danger of abasement and poverty being imposed upon him.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-52\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B048]<\/a><\/p><p>They also incurred God’s wrath by breaking covenants and transgressing limits: they disbelieved, grew insolent and killed the prophets without right. The cause was their rebellious persistence in disobedience, for sins draw one another on until they end in unbelief. In God is refuge from His displeasure and wrath.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when you said, “Moses, we cannot endure one food,” tiring of honey and meat and inclining towards your own bad sediment—that is, your familiar practices and bad desires, since they had been agricultural people. You said: ask your Lord to bring forth some of what He grows on earth: greens, cucumbers, lentils, garlic and onions. Moses asked: do you exchange the better—meat and honey—for the lower and baser—garlic, onions and the rest? Go down to any town and you will find your cravings, for these foods occur in villages and towns. Alternatively: return to Egypt, where you were humiliated and enslaved, and find your egoic shares and desires, for such shares and desires are bound to humiliation and abasement. Abasement and poverty were imposed upon them, clinging as a struck coin retains its impression and engraving. Abasement is the imposition of the tribute-tax ( الجزية ); poverty is poverty of the soul, even when materially wealthy. These were imposed because they were not content with the Real’s ordering or satisfied with His provision. Whoever is discontent with his allotted share and tires of the sameness of his sustenance [Translator’s note: Cairo I:114 prints وسلم , “and was free/safe,” apparently a corruption of وسئم , “and grew tired.” The rendering follows the latter conjecture, supported by the passage’s opening reference to weariness with manna and quails.] is in danger of abasement and poverty being imposed upon him. [B048] They also incurred God’s wrath by breaking covenants and transgressing limits: they disbelieved, grew insolent and killed the prophets without right. The cause was their rebellious persistence in disobedience, for sins draw one another on until they end in unbelief. In God is refuge from His displeasure and wrath."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Your own planning and the divine ordering","html":"<p>Whoever is not content with the eternal allotment and does not remain where divine power has stationed him, but inclines to his own shares and caprice and avidly pursues his aims and wishes, is asked: will you exchange the Real’s ordering, which is better, for your own planning, which is lower? Will you leave the ordering of the Wise, Knowing, Compassionate and Merciful for that of your weak, ignorant, paltry and ignoble intellect? You may plan something to your benefit, only to find it against you. Joy may come from where you expected harm, and harm from where you hoped for joy.<\/p><p>How excellent the poet’s saying:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">How often I sought a thing whose turning away You chose for me:<br>You have ever been kinder and more merciful to me than I.<br>I resolved that no thought should stir within the heart<br>unless You were placed first;<br>and that You should not see me where You forbade me,<br>so great and honoured are You in my heart.<\/blockquote><p>You who are discontent with your Master’s ordering and incline to attaining your own desire, descend to the earth of shares and appetites: there you will find the bad habits familiar to your soul. You whose soul clings to caprice and follows Satan, how can you exchange enduring dignity for humiliation and abasement? The following is recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Do not follow the soul in its caprice:<br>following caprice is abasement.<\/blockquote><p>In <i>al-Tanwīr<\/i>: “A beneficial observation. When the Children of Israel entered the wandering and were provided manna and quails, God had chosen that provision for them. It issued from pure favour, without toil or labour on their part. Their gross souls, through ingrained habit and absence from witnessing God’s ordering, returned to demanding what they had been accustomed to. They said, ‘Pray to your Lord for us to bring forth what the earth grows…’ He said, ‘Do you exchange the better for the inferior? Go down to a town, and you will have what you asked.’ Abasement and poverty were imposed upon them, and they incurred God’s wrath because they abandoned what God chose for them, inclining instead to their own choice.<\/p><p>“They were therefore reproached: ‘Do you exchange the better for the inferior?’ The outward explanation is: do you exchange manna and quails for garlic, lentils and onions? The two are unequal in pleasure and freedom from toil. The inward lesson is: do you exchange God’s will for you for your will for yourselves? The inferior is what you desire; the better is what God desires for you. ‘Go down to a town’: what you crave belongs only in towns. The mystery of the address is: descend from the heaven of entrusting yourselves and Our good ordering for you to the earth of your own planning and choice, marked by abasement and poverty because you choose alongside God’s choice and plan for yourselves alongside His ordering.” End of the relevant passage.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever is not content with the eternal allotment and does not remain where divine power has stationed him, but inclines to his own shares and caprice and avidly pursues his aims and wishes, is asked: will you exchange the Real’s ordering, which is better, for your own planning, which is lower? Will you leave the ordering of the Wise, Knowing, Compassionate and Merciful for that of your weak, ignorant, paltry and ignoble intellect? You may plan something to your benefit, only to find it against you. Joy may come from where you expected harm, and harm from where you hoped for joy. How excellent the poet’s saying: How often I sought a thing whose turning away You chose for me: You have ever been kinder and more merciful to me than I. I resolved that no thought should stir within the heart unless You were placed first; and that You should not see me where You forbade me, so great and honoured are You in my heart. You who are discontent with your Master’s ordering and incline to attaining your own desire, descend to the earth of shares and appetites: there you will find the bad habits familiar to your soul. You whose soul clings to caprice and follows Satan, how can you exchange enduring dignity for humiliation and abasement? The following is recited: Do not follow the soul in its caprice: following caprice is abasement. In al-Tanwīr : “A beneficial observation. When the Children of Israel entered the wandering and were provided manna and quails, God had chosen that provision for them. It issued from pure favour, without toil or labour on their part. Their gross souls, through ingrained habit and absence from witnessing God’s ordering, returned to demanding what they had been accustomed to. They said, ‘Pray to your Lord for us to bring forth what the earth grows…’ He said, ‘Do you exchange the better for the inferior? Go down to a town, and you will have what you asked.’ Abasement and poverty were imposed upon them, and they incurred God’s wrath because they abandoned what God chose for them, inclining instead to their own choice. “They were therefore reproached: ‘Do you exchange the better for the inferior?’ The outward explanation is: do you exchange manna and quails for garlic, lentils and onions? The two are unequal in pleasure and freedom from toil. The inward lesson is: do you exchange God’s will for you for your will for yourselves? The inferior is what you desire; the better is what God desires for you. ‘Go down to a town’: what you crave belongs only in towns. The mystery of the address is: descend from the heaven of entrusting yourselves and Our good ordering for you to the earth of your own planning and choice, marked by abasement and poverty because you choose alongside God’s choice and plan for yourselves alongside His ordering.” End of the relevant passage."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having reminded them of His blessing and rebuked their sins, the Real Most High encourages them towards Islam, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having reminded them of His blessing and rebuked their sins, the Real Most High encourages them towards Islam, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C113.1","page":113,"ar":"الحديث ذكره ابن الجوزي في الموضوعات.","en":"Ibn al-Jawzī mentions this hadith in al-Mawḍūʿāt.","anchor":"عليكم بالعدس فإنه مبارك مقدس …"},{"id":"C114.1","page":114,"ar":"في المخطوطات الثلاث (لأنك).","en":"The three manuscripts read “because You” (li-annaka).","anchor":"لكونك في قلبي كبيرا معظما"},{"id":"C115.1","page":115,"ar":"البيت للإمام البرعي.","en":"The verse is by Imam al-Buraʿī.","anchor":"لا تتبع النفس في هواها إن اتباع الهوى هوان"}],"english_characters":5690,"arabic_characters":4593},{"id":"q2-62","q":"2:62","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:62 · Faith, righteous action and four forms of divine care","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 115–116","cairo":"I:115–116","source_page_first":115,"source_page_last":116,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَالَّذِينَ هادُوا وَالنَّصارى وَالصَّابِئِينَ مَنْ آمَنَ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَعَمِلَ صالِحاً فَلَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (62)\nقلت: (إن) : ناصبة مؤكدة، وخبرها: جملة (مَن آمن) أو (فلهم أجرهم) . و (من آمن) : بدل من اسمها، أو محذوف، والموصول: مبتدأ أي: إن الذين آمنوا بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم لا خوف عليهم ولا هم يحزنون، والذين هادوا كذلك.\nو (هادوا) : تهودوا، أي: دخلوا في اليهودية. وسمّوا يهوداً إما نسبة لأبيهم الأكبر (يهوذا بن يعقوب) ، أو مِنْ هَادَ، إذَا تَابَ لأنهم تابوا من عبادة العجل.\nوالنصارى: جمع نصران، وسُموا بذلك إما لنصرهم المسيح عليه السلام، أو لسكناهم معه في قرية يقال لها:\n(نصران) ، والصابئون: طائفة من أهل الكتاب، خرجوا عن دين اليهودية وعبدوا الكواكب، يقال: صبا يصبو، إذا مال وخرج من دين إلى دين.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم والذين آمنوا بموسى، والذين آمنوا بعيسى- عليهما السلام-، والذين خرجوا عن دينهم وصبوا، مَنْ آمَنَ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وتبع محمداً صلّى الله عليه وسلّم وعمل بشريعته، فَلَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ إذا قدموا عليه بالنعيم المقيم، والنظر إلى وجهه الكريم، وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ حين يخاف الكفار، وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ حين يحزن المفرطون والأشرار إذ لا يلحقهم وبال ولا يفوتهم نوال. وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: إنَّ الذين آمنوا إيماناً لا يختلجه وهم، ولا يطرق ساحته شك ولا ريب، إما عن برهان قاطع، أو عن شهود ساطع، والذين تابوا عن هواجس الخواطر وغفلات الضمائر، والذين نصروا الدين، وشيّدوا منار شريعة المسلمين، والذين صبوا إلى الحبيب، ومالوا عن كل بعيد وقريب، فهؤلاء الذين سبقت لهم من الله العناية، وهَبَّتْ عليهم ريحُ الهداية، جمعوا بين تزيين البواطن بأنوار الإيقان، وتزيين الظواهر بأنواع الطاعة والإذعان، فلا جرم أنهم، إذا قدموا على ربهم، أجلَّ منصبهم، وأجزل ثوابهم، وأعلى مقامهم، فأولئك أولياء الله الذين لا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ.\nفالمخصوصون بالعناية أربعة: قوم أقامهم الحق تعالى لتنمية الإيمان وتربية الإيقان، إما عن دليل وبرهان- وهم أهل النظر والاعتبار، - وإما عن شهود وعيان- وهم أهل الشهود والاستبصار-، وقوم أقامهم الحق تعالى لتصفية نفوسهم وتزكية أحوالهم بالتوبة، والإقلاع عن كل وصف مذموم، وهم السائرون والطالبون، وقوم أقامهم لنصرة الدين وإظهار شريعة المسلمين، إما بتقرير قواعده أو جهاد معانده، وهم العلماء والمجاهدون، وقوم أقامهم لخدمته، وملأ قلوبهم بهيبته، وهم العُباد والزهاد، مالوا عن الشهوات وتأنسوا به في الخلوات، هجروا الأوطان وفارقوا الأحباب والإخوان، صبوا إلى محبة الحبيب وتلذذوا بمناجاة القريب، فهؤلاء المخصوصون بعين العناية، المحفوظون بغاية الرعاية، لا يخافون إذا خاف الناس، ولا يحزنون إذا حزن الناس. حققنا الله بمقام الجميع بمنِّه وكرمه. آمين.\nثم وبّخهم على نقض العهود، وعدم الوقوف مع الحدود، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَالَّذِينَ هادُوا وَالنَّصارى وَالصَّابِئِينَ مَنْ آمَنَ بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَعَمِلَ صالِحاً فَلَهُمْ أَجْرُهُمْ عِنْدَ رَبِّهِمْ وَلا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ (62)","quran_en":"62. Those who believe, those who are Jews, the Christians and the Sabians—whoever believes in God and the Last Day and acts righteously shall have their reward with their Lord; no fear shall be upon them, nor shall they grieve.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The conditional qualification and the communities named","html":"<p>I say: <i>inna<\/i> is emphatic and assigns the accusative to its noun. Its predicate may be the sentence beginning “whoever believes,” or “theirs is their reward,” with “whoever believes” an apposition to its noun. Alternatively, the predicate is omitted and the following relative expression begins a new nominal sentence: those who believe in Muḥammad have no fear and do not grieve; those who are Jews likewise.<\/p><p>“Those who became Jews” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هادوا<\/bdi>) means those who entered Judaism. They are called Jews either after their great ancestor Judah son of Jacob, or from <i>hāda<\/i>, “to repent,” because they repented of worshipping the calf.<\/p><p><i>Naṣārā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">النصارى<\/bdi>) is the plural of <i>naṣrān<\/i>. They are so called either because they supported (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نصروا<\/bdi>) the Messiah, peace be upon him, or because they lived with him in a village called Naṣrān. The Sabians are a group of the People of the Book who left Judaism and worshipped the stars. One says <i>ṣabā–yaṣbū<\/i> when someone inclines and passes from one religion to another.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-53\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B049]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: inna is emphatic and assigns the accusative to its noun. Its predicate may be the sentence beginning “whoever believes,” or “theirs is their reward,” with “whoever believes” an apposition to its noun. Alternatively, the predicate is omitted and the following relative expression begins a new nominal sentence: those who believe in Muḥammad have no fear and do not grieve; those who are Jews likewise. “Those who became Jews” ( هادوا ) means those who entered Judaism. They are called Jews either after their great ancestor Judah son of Jacob, or from hāda , “to repent,” because they repented of worshipping the calf. Naṣārā ( النصارى ) is the plural of naṣrān . They are so called either because they supported ( نصروا ) the Messiah, peace be upon him, or because they lived with him in a village called Naṣrān. The Sabians are a group of the People of the Book who left Judaism and worshipped the stars. One says ṣabā–yaṣbū when someone inclines and passes from one religion to another. [B049]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The commentator’s interpretation of the promised reward","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who believe in Muḥammad, those who believed in Moses or Jesus, peace be upon them, and those who left their religion and inclined away—whoever believes in God and the Last Day, follows Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, and acts according to his Law, has his reward with his Lord upon coming before Him: abiding bliss and the vision of His noble Face. They do not fear when unbelievers fear, nor grieve when the negligent and wicked grieve: no evil consequence reaches them, and no bounty escapes them. Through God is success.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-54\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B050]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: those who believe in Muḥammad, those who believed in Moses or Jesus, peace be upon them, and those who left their religion and inclined away—whoever believes in God and the Last Day, follows Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, and acts according to his Law, has his reward with his Lord upon coming before Him: abiding bliss and the vision of His noble Face. They do not fear when unbelievers fear, nor grieve when the negligent and wicked grieve: no evil consequence reaches them, and no bounty escapes them. Through God is success. [B050]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Four groups specially embraced by providence","html":"<p>Those who believe with a faith untouched by illusion, doubt or uncertainty, whether through decisive proof or radiant witnessing; those who repent of whispering thoughts and inward heedlessness; those who support religion and build high the beacon of the Muslims’ Law; and those who incline towards the Beloved, turning from every near or distant other—these are those whom God’s antecedent care embraces and the breeze of guidance visits. They unite adorning the inward being with certainty’s lights and the outward being with obedience and submission. Necessarily, when they come before their Lord, He honours their standing, makes their reward abundant, and raises their station. These are God’s friends, upon whom is no fear and who do not grieve.<\/p><p>Those specially embraced by providence form four groups. One He establishes to nurture faith and cultivate certainty, through evidence and proof—the people of reflection and consideration—or through witnessing and direct vision—the people of witnessing and insight. Another He establishes to purify their souls and refine their states through repentance and abandoning every blameworthy quality: these are wayfarers and seekers. Another He establishes to support religion and manifest the Muslims’ Law, through expounding its principles or striving against its adversaries: these are scholars and fighters in God’s path. Another He establishes in His service, filling their hearts with awe: these are worshippers and ascetics. They turn from desires, find intimacy with Him in retreat, abandon homelands and part from beloveds and brethren. They incline towards the Beloved’s love and delight in conversing with the Near One. These are singled out by providential care and protected with the utmost solicitude. They neither fear when people fear nor grieve when people grieve. May God realize us in all their stations through His favour and generosity. Amen.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Those who believe with a faith untouched by illusion, doubt or uncertainty, whether through decisive proof or radiant witnessing; those who repent of whispering thoughts and inward heedlessness; those who support religion and build high the beacon of the Muslims’ Law; and those who incline towards the Beloved, turning from every near or distant other—these are those whom God’s antecedent care embraces and the breeze of guidance visits. They unite adorning the inward being with certainty’s lights and the outward being with obedience and submission. Necessarily, when they come before their Lord, He honours their standing, makes their reward abundant, and raises their station. These are God’s friends, upon whom is no fear and who do not grieve. Those specially embraced by providence form four groups. One He establishes to nurture faith and cultivate certainty, through evidence and proof—the people of reflection and consideration—or through witnessing and direct vision—the people of witnessing and insight. Another He establishes to purify their souls and refine their states through repentance and abandoning every blameworthy quality: these are wayfarers and seekers. Another He establishes to support religion and manifest the Muslims’ Law, through expounding its principles or striving against its adversaries: these are scholars and fighters in God’s path. Another He establishes in His service, filling their hearts with awe: these are worshippers and ascetics. They turn from desires, find intimacy with Him in retreat, abandon homelands and part from beloveds and brethren. They incline towards the Beloved’s love and delight in conversing with the Near One. These are singled out by providential care and protected with the utmost solicitude. They neither fear when people fear nor grieve when people grieve. May God realize us in all their stations through His favour and generosity. Amen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He rebukes their breaking of covenants and failure to remain within the limits, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He rebukes their breaking of covenants and failure to remain within the limits, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3589,"arabic_characters":2610},{"id":"q2-63-66","q":"2:63–66","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:63–66 · The covenant, the raised Mount and the Sabbath","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 116–118","cairo":"I:116–118","source_page_first":116,"source_page_last":118,"ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ وَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَكُمُ الطُّورَ خُذُوا ما آتَيْناكُمْ بِقُوَّةٍ وَاذْكُرُوا ما فِيهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَّقُونَ (63) ثُمَّ تَوَلَّيْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ فَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَتُهُ لَكُنْتُمْ مِنَ الْخاسِرِينَ (64) \nوَلَقَدْ عَلِمْتُمُ الَّذِينَ اعْتَدَوْا مِنْكُمْ فِي السَّبْتِ فَقُلْنا لَهُمْ كُونُوا قِرَدَةً خاسِئِينَ (65) فَجَعَلْناها نَكالاً لِما بَيْنَ يَدَيْها وَما خَلْفَها وَمَوْعِظَةً لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (66)\n\nقلت: (لولا) : حرف امتناع لوجود ، تلزم الدخول على المبتدأ، وخبرها واجب الحذف عند سيبويه، أي:\nلولا فضل الله عليكم ورحمته موجودان، وقال الكوفيون: فاعل بمحذوف: أي: لولا أن ثبت فضل الله عليكم ورحمته، و (لكنتم) : جوابها.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا يا بني إسرائيل حين أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ أن تقبلوا تكاليف التوراة، وكانت شاقة عليهم، فلما أبيتم قبولها، قلعنا الطور، ورفعناه فوقكم على مقدار عسكركم، كالظلة، وقلنا لكم:\nخُذُوا ما آتَيْناكُمْ من التوراة بجد واجتهاد، وَاذْكُرُوا ما فِيهِ من الوعظ والتذكير لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَّقُونَ الله، فتفوزون بالخير الكثير، فقبلتم ذلك كرهاً ثُمَّ تَوَلَّيْتُمْ وأعرضتم بعد ذلك، فسفكتم الدماء، وقتلتم الأنبياء، فَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ بتوفيقكم للتوبة، وَرَحْمَتُهُ بقبولها منكم، لخسرتم الدنيا والآخرة.\nوَلَقَدْ عَلِمْتُمُ ما جرى للذين اعْتَدَوْا مِنْكُمْ فِي السَّبْتِ في زمن داود عليه السلام، وذلك في قرية يقال لها: «أيْلة» ، كانت على شاطىء البحر، وقد نُهوا عن الاصطياد يوم السبت، فكانت الحيتان تخرج يوم السبت شُرَّعاً، فتُخْرِج خراطيمها للبر، فإذا كان يوم الأحد دخلت في البحر، فحفروا حياضا، وشرعوا إليها جداول، فكانت الحيتان تدخلها يوم السبت فيصطادونها يوم الأحد، فلمَّا لمْ يُعاقبوا على ذلك أحلُّوا يوم السبت، فانقسمت القرية على ثلاث فرق: قوم نهوا، وقوم سكتوا، وقوم اصطادوا، فمُسِخ من اصطاد قردة وخنازير الشُّبان قردة، والشيوخ خنازير، فبقوا ثلاثة أيام وماتوا. فجعلنا تلك الفعلة التي فعلنا بهم- نَكالًا وزجراً لِما بَيْنَ يَدَيْها في زمانها، وما خلفها من يأتي بعدها، وَمَوْعِظَةً: وتذكيراً لِلْمُتَّقِينَ من أمة محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلّم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن المريدين إذا دخلوا في يد شيخ، وأخذوا عنه العهد، حملهم من أعباء التكليف وخرق العوائد ما تموت به نفوسهم، وتحيا به قلوبهم، كذبح النفوس وحط الرؤوس ودفع الفلوس، فإذا هموا بالتقصير، ظلل عليهم جبل همته، وأدار عليهم يد حفظه ورعايته، ولذلك قال الشيخ أبو الحسن: (والله لا يكون الشيخ شيخاً حتى تكون يده مع الفقير أينما ذهب) . والمراد باليد: الهمة والحفظ، ولا يزال الشيخ يراسلهم بهذه التكاليف، ويحضّهم على الأخذ بها، والاجتهاد في العمل بها، حتى تموت نفوسهم وتحيا قلوبهم، وترسخ معرفتهم، وتكمل تربيتهم، فحينئذٍ ينتقلون إلى روح وريحان في جنات الشهود والعيان.\nقلت: وقد كان شيخنا يرسل لنا البطاقات في حال البدايات، فما كنت أفتحها حتى ترتعد نفسي مما فيها، لأنها تعلم أنه ما يُرسل لها إلا ما فيه موتها، فلولا فضل الله علينا ورحمته- حتى قوانا على العمل بما فيها- لكنا من الخاسرين، ولقد أخطأت العناية قوماً، فتعدوا حدود الشيوخ، أو خرجوا عن دائرتهم قبل كمال تربيتهم، فمسخت قلوبهم، وانمحت من ديوان الولاية رسومهم، جعل الله ذلك عبرة لغيرهم، وزاجراً لمن حذا حذوهم، نعوذ بالله من السلب بعد العطاء، وكفران النعم وحرمان الرضى، وبالله التوفيق وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم وبّخهم بما فعل أسلافهم من قتل النفس والتشغيب على نبيهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ وَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَكُمُ الطُّورَ خُذُوا ما آتَيْناكُمْ بِقُوَّةٍ وَاذْكُرُوا ما فِيهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَّقُونَ (63) ثُمَّ تَوَلَّيْتُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ فَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَرَحْمَتُهُ لَكُنْتُمْ مِنَ الْخاسِرِينَ (64) وَلَقَدْ عَلِمْتُمُ الَّذِينَ اعْتَدَوْا مِنْكُمْ فِي السَّبْتِ فَقُلْنا لَهُمْ كُونُوا قِرَدَةً خاسِئِينَ (65) فَجَعَلْناها نَكالاً لِما بَيْنَ يَدَيْها وَما خَلْفَها وَمَوْعِظَةً لِلْمُتَّقِينَ (66)","quran_en":"63. When We took your covenant and raised the Mount above you: “Hold firmly to what We have given you, and remember what is in it, so that you may be mindful of God.” 64. Then, after that, you turned away. Had it not been for God’s bounty and mercy towards you, you would have been among the losers. 65. You knew those among you who transgressed concerning the Sabbath. We said to them, “Be apes, despised!” 66. We made it a deterrent for those present and those coming after, and an admonition for the God-conscious.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Lawlā and the unfulfilled consequence","html":"<p>I say: <i>lawlā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لولا<\/bdi>) is a particle signifying that one thing fails to occur because another exists (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حرف امتناع لوجود<\/bdi>). It necessarily precedes a subject, whose predicate, according to Sībawayh, must be omitted: “had God’s bounty and mercy towards you not been existent…” The Kufans take what follows as the subject of an implied verb: “had God’s bounty and mercy not been established…” “You would have been” is its response.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: lawlā ( لولا ) is a particle signifying that one thing fails to occur because another exists ( حرف امتناع لوجود ). It necessarily precedes a subject, whose predicate, according to Sībawayh, must be omitted: “had God’s bounty and mercy towards you not been existent…” The Kufans take what follows as the subject of an implied verb: “had God’s bounty and mercy not been established…” “You would have been” is its response."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Accepting the obligation and subsequently turning away","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when We bound you to accept the Torah’s obligations, which were burdensome to you. When you refused, We uprooted the Mount and raised it above you like a canopy, covering the extent of your camp. We told you: take the Torah We have given with seriousness and effort, and remember its admonitions and reminders, so that you may fear God and attain abundant good. You accepted unwillingly, then turned away, shedding blood and killing prophets. Were it not for God’s favour in enabling your repentance and His mercy in accepting it, you would have lost both this world and the Hereafter.<\/p><p>You knew what befell those who violated the Sabbath in David’s time, peace be upon him, in a coastal town called Ayla. They had been forbidden to fish on the Sabbath. On that day the fish appeared openly, extending their snouts towards the shore; on Sunday they returned to sea. The people dug pools and cut channels leading to them. Fish entered on Saturday and were caught on Sunday. When no punishment followed, they made fishing on the Sabbath lawful for themselves. The town divided into three groups: those who forbade, those who remained silent, and those who fished. Those who fished were transformed into apes and swine: the young into apes, the old into swine. They remained three days and died. We made that act a chastisement and deterrent for those present at its time and those who came after, and an admonition for the God-conscious of Muḥammad’s community, God bless him and grant him peace.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-55\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B051]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when We bound you to accept the Torah’s obligations, which were burdensome to you. When you refused, We uprooted the Mount and raised it above you like a canopy, covering the extent of your camp. We told you: take the Torah We have given with seriousness and effort, and remember its admonitions and reminders, so that you may fear God and attain abundant good. You accepted unwillingly, then turned away, shedding blood and killing prophets. Were it not for God’s favour in enabling your repentance and His mercy in accepting it, you would have lost both this world and the Hereafter. You knew what befell those who violated the Sabbath in David’s time, peace be upon him, in a coastal town called Ayla. They had been forbidden to fish on the Sabbath. On that day the fish appeared openly, extending their snouts towards the shore; on Sunday they returned to sea. The people dug pools and cut channels leading to them. Fish entered on Saturday and were caught on Sunday. When no punishment followed, they made fishing on the Sabbath lawful for themselves. The town divided into three groups: those who forbade, those who remained silent, and those who fished. Those who fished were transformed into apes and swine: the young into apes, the old into swine. They remained three days and died. We made that act a chastisement and deterrent for those present at its time and those who came after, and an admonition for the God-conscious of Muḥammad’s community, God bless him and grant him peace. [B051]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The guide’s protective aspiration","html":"<p>When disciples enter a shaykh’s hand and take the covenant from him, he places upon them burdens and breaks their customary habits in ways that cause their lower selves to die and their hearts to live: slaughtering selfhood, lowering their heads, and parting with money. If they incline to neglect, he overshadows them with the mountain of his aspiration and surrounds them with the hand of his protection and care. Hence Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan said: “By God, a shaykh is not a shaykh until his hand accompanies the spiritual poor wherever he goes.” The hand means aspiration and protection, not the bodily hand. The shaykh continues to send these disciplines, urging them to take them up and work diligently with them, until their lower selves die, their hearts live, their knowledge becomes rooted and their formation complete. They then pass to repose and fragrant ease in the gardens of witnessing and direct vision.<\/p><p>I say: our shaykh used to send us written slips in the beginning. Before opening one my lower self would tremble at its contents, knowing that he sent only what would cause its death. Were it not for God’s bounty and mercy, strengthening us to act upon them, we would have been losers. Some were missed by providential care: they exceeded their shaykhs’ limits or left their circle before completing their formation. Their hearts were transformed and their inscriptions erased from the register of friendship with God. God made this a lesson to others and a restraint upon those following their example. We seek refuge in God from deprivation after bestowal, ingratitude for blessings and loss of good pleasure. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When disciples enter a shaykh’s hand and take the covenant from him, he places upon them burdens and breaks their customary habits in ways that cause their lower selves to die and their hearts to live: slaughtering selfhood, lowering their heads, and parting with money. If they incline to neglect, he overshadows them with the mountain of his aspiration and surrounds them with the hand of his protection and care. Hence Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan said: “By God, a shaykh is not a shaykh until his hand accompanies the spiritual poor wherever he goes.” The hand means aspiration and protection, not the bodily hand. The shaykh continues to send these disciplines, urging them to take them up and work diligently with them, until their lower selves die, their hearts live, their knowledge becomes rooted and their formation complete. They then pass to repose and fragrant ease in the gardens of witnessing and direct vision. I say: our shaykh used to send us written slips in the beginning. Before opening one my lower self would tremble at its contents, knowing that he sent only what would cause its death. Were it not for God’s bounty and mercy, strengthening us to act upon them, we would have been losers. Some were missed by providential care: they exceeded their shaykhs’ limits or left their circle before completing their formation. Their hearts were transformed and their inscriptions erased from the register of friendship with God. God made this a lesson to others and a restraint upon those following their example. We seek refuge in God from deprivation after bestowal, ingratitude for blessings and loss of good pleasure. Through God is success, and He guides to the even way."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He rebukes what their forebears did in killing a soul and disputing with their prophet, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He rebukes what their forebears did in killing a soul and disputing with their prophet, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C117.1","page":117,"ar":"أي: امتناع شيء لوجود غيره.","en":"That is, the non-occurrence of one thing because another exists.","anchor":"حرف امتناع لوجود"}],"english_characters":3776,"arabic_characters":3061},{"id":"q2-67-71","q":"2:67–71","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:67–71 · The cow and the burden of needless questioning","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 118–119","cairo":"I:118–119","source_page_first":118,"source_page_last":119,"ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ مُوسى لِقَوْمِهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَأْمُرُكُمْ أَنْ تَذْبَحُوا بَقَرَةً قالُوا أَتَتَّخِذُنا هُزُواً قالَ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ أَنْ أَكُونَ مِنَ الْجاهِلِينَ (67) قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما هِيَ قالَ إِنَّهُ يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ لا فارِضٌ وَلا بِكْرٌ عَوانٌ بَيْنَ ذلِكَ فَافْعَلُوا ما تُؤْمَرُونَ (68) قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما لَوْنُها قالَ إِنَّهُ يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ صَفْراءُ فاقِعٌ لَوْنُها تَسُرُّ النَّاظِرِينَ (69) قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما هِيَ إِنَّ الْبَقَرَ تَشابَهَ عَلَيْنا وَإِنَّا إِنْ شاءَ اللَّهُ لَمُهْتَدُونَ (70) قالَ إِنَّهُ يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ لا ذَلُولٌ تُثِيرُ الْأَرْضَ وَلا تَسْقِي الْحَرْثَ مُسَلَّمَةٌ لا شِيَةَ فِيها قالُوا الْآنَ جِئْتَ بِالْحَقِّ فَذَبَحُوها وَما كادُوا يَفْعَلُونَ (71)\nقلت: الفارض: المسنة التي لا تلد، يقال: فرضت البقرة تفرض فروضاً، إذا أسنت. والبكر: الصغيرة التي لم تلد، العوان: المتوسطة بين المسنة والصغيرة، والفاقع: الناصع الصفرة، يقال: أصفر فاقع، وأسود حالك: أي:\nشديد السواد. وأصل شية: وِشْيَة، كعدة، حذفت فاؤها وعوض عنها التاء، والوشي: الرقم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا يا بني إسرائيل حين قالَ مُوسى لِقَوْمِهِ لما تخاصموا إليه في قتيل وجد في قرية ولم يدر قاتله، وذلك أن رجلاً فقيراً من بني إسرائيل قتل قريباً له كان موسراً ليرثه، ثم رماه في قرية أخرى، ثم ذهب يطلب دمه، فترافعوا إلى موسى عليه السلام فقال لهم بوحي: إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَأْمُرُكُمْ أَنْ تَذْبَحُوا بَقَرَةً، وأبهم الأمر عليهم، قالُوا أَتَتَّخِذُنا هُزُواً أي: مهزوءاً بنا، حيث نسألك عن بيان القاتل وأنت تأمرنا أن نذبح بقرة، وهذا من تعنتهم وسوء أدبهم. قالَ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ أَنْ أَكُونَ مِنَ الْجاهِلِينَ إذ لا يستهزئ بأمر الدين إلا الجاهل.\nفلما رأوا جدَّه قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا مَا هِيَ، هل هي كبيرة أو صغيرة أو متوسطة؟ قالَ إِنَّهُ يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ لاَّ فارِضٌ أي: كبيرة، وَلا بِكْرٌ أي: ولا صغيرة، عَوانٌ متوسطة بين ما ذكر من الصغر والكبر، فَافْعَلُوا مَا تُؤْمَرُونَ، فإن الله يُبين لكم القاتل، قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما لَوْنُها، أهي حمراء أو سوداء أو صفراء؟ قالَ إِنَّهُ تعالى يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ صَفْراءُ فاقِعٌ لَوْنُها ناصع صفرتها تَسُرُّ النَّاظِرِينَ لسمنها وبهجة لونها، قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما هِيَ، فإن البقر الصفر كثير، وقد تشابه علينا أمرها؟ قالَ إِنَّهُ تعالى يقول: إنها مسلمة من العمل ليست ذلولاً، أي: مذللة بالعمل لا تُثِيرُ أي: تقلب الْأَرْضَ وَلا تَسْقِي الْحَرْثَ بالسانية . مُسَلَّمَةٌ من العيوب كلها، لاَّ شِيَةَ فِيها أي: لا رقم فيها يخالف الصفرة.\nفلما تبين لهم الأمر قالُوا الْآنَ جِئْتَ بِالْحَقِّ الواضح، فوجدوها عند شاب كان بيد أمه، قد استودعها له أبوه في غيضة ، فاشتروها منه بملء جلدها ذهباً، أو بوزنها، فَذَبَحُوها، وضربوا القتيل بجزء منها، فجلس وعروقه تسيل دماً، وقال: قتلني ابن عم لي، ثم رجع، وَما كادُوا يَفْعَلُونَ لكثرة ترددهم، أو لفحش غلوها. قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لو ذبحوا أدنى بقرة لكفتهم لكن شددوا فشدد الله عليهم» .\nثم ذكر أول القصة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قالَ مُوسى لِقَوْمِهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَأْمُرُكُمْ أَنْ تَذْبَحُوا بَقَرَةً قالُوا أَتَتَّخِذُنا هُزُواً قالَ أَعُوذُ بِاللَّهِ أَنْ أَكُونَ مِنَ الْجاهِلِينَ (67) قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما هِيَ قالَ إِنَّهُ يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ لا فارِضٌ وَلا بِكْرٌ عَوانٌ بَيْنَ ذلِكَ فَافْعَلُوا ما تُؤْمَرُونَ (68) قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما لَوْنُها قالَ إِنَّهُ يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ صَفْراءُ فاقِعٌ لَوْنُها تَسُرُّ النَّاظِرِينَ (69) قالُوا ادْعُ لَنا رَبَّكَ يُبَيِّنْ لَنا ما هِيَ إِنَّ الْبَقَرَ تَشابَهَ عَلَيْنا وَإِنَّا إِنْ شاءَ اللَّهُ لَمُهْتَدُونَ (70) قالَ إِنَّهُ يَقُولُ إِنَّها بَقَرَةٌ لا ذَلُولٌ تُثِيرُ الْأَرْضَ وَلا تَسْقِي الْحَرْثَ مُسَلَّمَةٌ لا شِيَةَ فِيها قالُوا الْآنَ جِئْتَ بِالْحَقِّ فَذَبَحُوها وَما كادُوا يَفْعَلُونَ (71)","quran_en":"67. When Moses said to his people, “God commands you to slaughter a cow,” they said, “Do you make a mockery of us?” He said, “I seek refuge in God from being among the ignorant.” 68. They said, “Pray to your Lord for us to explain what it is.” He said, “He says it is a cow neither old nor young, but midway between the two. Do what you are commanded.” 69. They said, “Pray to your Lord for us to explain its colour.” He said, “He says it is a yellow cow, bright in colour, pleasing to those who look.” 70. They said, “Pray to your Lord for us to explain what it is, for the cows look alike to us; God willing, we shall be guided.” 71. He said, “He says it is a cow not trained to plough the earth or water the crops, sound and without a mark.” They said, “Now you have brought the truth.” They slaughtered it, though they had nearly failed to do so.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The cow’s age, colour and markings","html":"<p>I say: <i>fāriḍ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فارض<\/bdi>) is an old cow that no longer bears young; <i>faraḍat al-baqara–tafriḍu–furūḍan<\/i> means it has grown old. <i>Bikr<\/i> is a young cow that has not calved. <i>ʿAwān<\/i> is intermediate between old and young. <i>Fāqiʿ<\/i> means brilliantly yellow; one says “vivid yellow” and “pitch black,” the latter meaning intensely black. <i>Shiya<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شية<\/bdi>) derives from <i>wishya<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وِشية<\/bdi>), like <i>ʿida<\/i> from its root: the first radical is dropped and compensated by the final tāʾ. <i>Washy<\/i> is a coloured pattern.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: fāriḍ ( فارض ) is an old cow that no longer bears young; faraḍat al-baqara–tafriḍu–furūḍan means it has grown old. Bikr is a young cow that has not calved. ʿAwān is intermediate between old and young. Fāqiʿ means brilliantly yellow; one says “vivid yellow” and “pitch black,” the latter meaning intensely black. Shiya ( شية ) derives from wishya ( وِشية ), like ʿida from its root: the first radical is dropped and compensated by the final tāʾ. Washy is a coloured pattern."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The murder, the questions and the eventual obedience","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when Moses spoke to his people after they disputed before him about a slain man found in a village, whose killer was unknown. A poor Israelite had killed a wealthy relative to inherit from him, cast his body into another village, and gone to demand vengeance for him. They brought their case before Moses, who told them through revelation: God commands you to slaughter a cow. The command left its description unspecified. They asked, “Do you make us objects of mockery?” We ask you to disclose the killer, and you command us to slaughter a cow! This was obstinacy and ill courtesy. He replied, “I seek refuge in God from being among the ignorant,” for only an ignorant person mocks a matter of religion.<\/p><p>Seeing his seriousness, they asked: pray to your Lord to explain whether it is old, young or intermediate. He replied: He says it is neither old nor young, but midway between the two. Do what you are commanded, and God will disclose the killer. They asked: pray to your Lord to explain its colour—is it red, black or yellow? He replied: He says it is yellow, brilliant in yellowness, pleasing those who look through its plumpness and the beauty of its colour. They asked again what it was, since yellow cows were numerous and the matter seemed confused.<\/p><p>He replied: He says it is free of labour, not broken to work. It does not turn the earth in ploughing or water crops by drawing water. It is sound, free of every defect, with no marking differing from its yellow colour. When the matter became clear, they said, “Now you have brought the clear truth.” They found it with a young man under his mother’s care; his father had placed it in safekeeping for him in a thicket. They bought it for its hide full of gold, or for its weight in gold. They slaughtered it and struck the dead man with part of it. He sat up, blood flowing from his veins, and said, “A paternal cousin of mine killed me,” then returned to death. They had nearly failed to do it, through their repeated hesitation or its exorbitant price. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: “Had they slaughtered the least cow, it would have sufficed; but they made things difficult, so God made them difficult for them.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-56\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B052]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Children of Israel, remember when Moses spoke to his people after they disputed before him about a slain man found in a village, whose killer was unknown. A poor Israelite had killed a wealthy relative to inherit from him, cast his body into another village, and gone to demand vengeance for him. They brought their case before Moses, who told them through revelation: God commands you to slaughter a cow. The command left its description unspecified. They asked, “Do you make us objects of mockery?” We ask you to disclose the killer, and you command us to slaughter a cow! This was obstinacy and ill courtesy. He replied, “I seek refuge in God from being among the ignorant,” for only an ignorant person mocks a matter of religion. Seeing his seriousness, they asked: pray to your Lord to explain whether it is old, young or intermediate. He replied: He says it is neither old nor young, but midway between the two. Do what you are commanded, and God will disclose the killer. They asked: pray to your Lord to explain its colour—is it red, black or yellow? He replied: He says it is yellow, brilliant in yellowness, pleasing those who look through its plumpness and the beauty of its colour. They asked again what it was, since yellow cows were numerous and the matter seemed confused. He replied: He says it is free of labour, not broken to work. It does not turn the earth in ploughing or water crops by drawing water. It is sound, free of every defect, with no marking differing from its yellow colour. When the matter became clear, they said, “Now you have brought the clear truth.” They found it with a young man under his mother’s care; his father had placed it in safekeeping for him in a thicket. They bought it for its hide full of gold, or for its weight in gold. They slaughtered it and struck the dead man with part of it. He sat up, blood flowing from his veins, and said, “A paternal cousin of mine killed me,” then returned to death. They had nearly failed to do it, through their repeated hesitation or its exorbitant price. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: “Had they slaughtered the least cow, it would have sufficed; but they made things difficult, so God made them difficult for them.” [B052]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then recounts the beginning of the story, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then recounts the beginning of the story, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C118.1","page":118,"ar":"في الأصول: (نذبحوا).","en":"The source copies read: “nadhbaḥū” (نذبحوا).","anchor":"أن نذبح بقرة"},{"id":"C119.1","page":119,"ar":"السانية: الساقية.","en":"Al-sāniya: the water-drawing wheel.","anchor":"بالسانية"},{"id":"C119.2","page":119,"ar":"الموضع الذي يكثر فيه الشجر.","en":"A place where trees grow thickly.","anchor":"غيضة"}],"english_characters":2805,"arabic_characters":2843},{"id":"q2-72-73","q":"2:72–73","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:72–73 · The slain man restored and the limits of legal inference","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 119–121","cairo":"I:119–121","source_page_first":119,"source_page_last":121,"ar":"وَإِذْ قَتَلْتُمْ نَفْساً فَادَّارَأْتُمْ فِيها وَاللَّهُ مُخْرِجٌ ما كُنْتُمْ تَكْتُمُونَ (72) فَقُلْنا اضْرِبُوهُ بِبَعْضِها كَذلِكَ يُحْيِ اللَّهُ الْمَوْتى وَيُرِيكُمْ آياتِهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ (73)\nقلت: حق هذه الآية أن تتقدم قبل قوله: إِنَّ اللَّهَ يَأْمُرُكُمْ ... وإنما أخَّرها الحق تعالى ليتوجه العتاب إليهم مرتين على ترك المسارعة لامتثال أمر نبيهم، وعلى قتل النفس، ولو قدمها لكانت قصة واحدة بتوبيخ واحد.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَاذكروا إِذْ قَتَلْتُمْ نَفْساً حرصاً على الدنيا فَادَّارَأْتُمْ أي: تدافعتم في شأنها، كل قرية تدفع عنها، وَاللَّهُ تعالى مُخْرِجٌ ومبين مَّا كُنْتُمْ تَكْتُمُونَ من القتل، ومن قتله، فَقُلْنا\n: اضربوا القتيل أو قبره بِبَعْضِها\nقيل: اللسان، وقيل: القلب، وقيل: الفخذ أو الذنب، فضربوه فحيى، وأخبر بقاتله كما تقدم، كَذلِكَ\nأي: كما أحيا هذا القتيل، يُحْيِ اللَّهُ الْمَوْتى\nمن قبورها وَيُرِيكُمْ آياتِهِ\nالدالة على قدرته، لَعَلَّكُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ\nفتعلمون أنَّ مَن قَدَر على إحياء نفس واحدة يقدر على إحياء الأنفس كلها.\nواستدلت المالكية بالقصة على التدمية الحمراء ، وهي قبول قول القتيل قبل موته بأن فلاناً قتله، وفيه نظر لأن هذا حيى بعد موته فلا يتطرقه الكذب، واستدلت أيضاً على حرمان القاتل من الإرث، وفيه نظر لأن هذه شريعة من قبلنا يتطرقها النسخ، لكن ثبت في الحديث أنه لا يرث. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: إذا أمر الشيخ المريدين بذبح نفوسهم بخرق عوائدها، فمن تردد منهم في فعل ما تموت به نفسه، كان ذلك دليلاً على قلة صدقه وضعف نهايته، ومن بادر منهم إلى قتلها دلّ ذلك على صدقه وفلاحه ونجح نهايته، فإذا ماتت النفس بالكلية حييت روحه بالمعرفة والمشاهدة الدائمة، فلا موت بعدها أبداً، قال تعالى:\nلا يَذُوقُونَ فِيهَا الْمَوْتَ إِلَّا الْمَوْتَةَ الْأُولى، وأما الموت الطبيعي فإنما هو انتقال من مقام إلى مقام، ومن وطن ضيق إلى وطن واسع، وأنشدوا:\nلا تظُنُّوا الموتَ موتاً إنهُ ... لَحَيَاةٌ، وَهْو غايةُ المنَى\nلا تَرُعْكُم هَجْمَةُ الموتِ فَما ... هُو إلا انْتِقَالٌ مِنْ هنَا\nفاخلعوا الأجساد من أنفسكم ... تُبْصِرُوا الحقَّ عيَاناً بَيِّنَا\nقلت: والسيف الذي يُجْهز على النفس ويسرع قتلها هو الذل والفقر، فمن ذلّ نفسه بين أبناء جنسه، وخرق عوائد نفسه، وزهد في الدنيا، ماتت نفسه في طرفة عين، وحيِيَتْ روحه، وظفر بِقُرَّةِ العين، وهي معرفة مولاه، والغيبة عما سواه.\nوكمال الوقت في ذبح النفس أن تكون متوسطة بين الصغر والكبر، فإن الصغيرة جدّاً لا يؤمن عليها الرجوع، والكبيرة جدّاً قد يصعب عليها النزوع، كاملة الأوصاف بحسن الزهد والعفاف، تسر الناظرين لبهجة منظرها وحسن طلعتها، وكذلك مَن كان من أهل الشهود والنظرة، تسحر مشاهدته القلوب، ويسوقها بسرعة إلى حضرة علام الغيوب، لما أقيم به من مشاهدته الملكوت، حتى إن من لا حظه تناسى أحوال البشرية، واستولت عليه أنوار الروحانية، وغاب في ذكر الحبيب عن البعيد والقريب، كما في الحديث: «أولياء اللَّهِ مَنْ إِذا رُؤوا ذُكر الله» وتكون أيضاً هذه النفس غير مذللة بطلب الدنيا والحرص عليها، مسلمة لا عيب فيها، ولا رِقَّ لشيء من الأثر عليها، فحينئذٍ تصلح للحضرة، وتتمتع بنعيم الشهود والنظرة، لم يبق لخصم الفَرْقِ معها تدارؤٌ ولا نزاع، بل أقر الخصم وارتفع النزاع.\nثم وبّخهم على عدم تأثير هذه المعجزة فى قلوبهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ قَتَلْتُمْ نَفْساً فَادَّارَأْتُمْ فِيها وَاللَّهُ مُخْرِجٌ ما كُنْتُمْ تَكْتُمُونَ (72) فَقُلْنا اضْرِبُوهُ بِبَعْضِها كَذلِكَ يُحْيِ اللَّهُ الْمَوْتى وَيُرِيكُمْ آياتِهِ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ (73)","quran_en":"72. When you killed a person and disputed about it, God was bringing out what you concealed. 73. We said, “Strike him with part of it.” Thus God gives life to the dead and shows you His signs, so that you may understand.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Why the beginning of the story is placed afterwards","html":"<p>I say: in narrative sequence this verse belongs before “God commands you…” The Real Most High postpones it so that the reproach reaches them twice: for failing to hasten to obey their prophet, and for killing a soul. Had it been placed first, there would have been one story and one rebuke.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: in narrative sequence this verse belongs before “God commands you…” The Real Most High postpones it so that the reproach reaches them twice: for failing to hasten to obey their prophet, and for killing a soul. Had it been placed first, there would have been one story and one rebuke."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The sign of resurrection","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when you killed a soul through greed for worldly gain, then disputed concerning it, each village repelling the accusation. God brings out and makes clear what you concealed of the killing and its perpetrator. We said: strike the dead man—or his grave—with part of the cow, said to be its tongue, heart, thigh or tail. They struck him; he revived and named his killer, as already mentioned. Just as He revived this slain man, God gives life to the dead from their graves and shows you the signs of His power, so that you may understand: He who can revive one soul can revive them all.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when you killed a soul through greed for worldly gain, then disputed concerning it, each village repelling the accusation. God brings out and makes clear what you concealed of the killing and its perpetrator. We said: strike the dead man—or his grave—with part of the cow, said to be its tongue, heart, thigh or tail. They struck him; he revived and named his killer, as already mentioned. Just as He revived this slain man, God gives life to the dead from their graves and shows you the signs of His power, so that you may understand: He who can revive one soul can revive them all."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A dying accusation and the killer’s inheritance","html":"<p>The Mālikīs adduce this story for the bloodied victim’s accusation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التدمية الحمراء<\/bdi>), accepting the victim’s statement before death that a particular person killed him. This requires examination, because this man revived after death, and lying could not enter his statement. They also use it to establish that the killer is deprived of inheritance. This too requires examination, because it is the law of those before us, which may be subject to abrogation. The rule that he does not inherit is, however, established in hadith. And God Most High knows best.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-57\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B053]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Mālikīs adduce this story for the bloodied victim’s accusation ( التدمية الحمراء ), accepting the victim’s statement before death that a particular person killed him. This requires examination, because this man revived after death, and lying could not enter his statement. They also use it to establish that the killer is deprived of inheritance. This too requires examination, because it is the law of those before us, which may be subject to abrogation. The rule that he does not inherit is, however, established in hadith. And God Most High knows best. [B053]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The death of selfhood and the fitness of the soul","html":"<p>If a shaykh commands disciples to slaughter their lower selves by breaking customary habits, hesitation over what brings the lower self’s death indicates scant sincerity and a weak final attainment. Haste to overcome it indicates sincerity, success and a sound end. When the lower self dies entirely, the spirit lives through knowledge and perpetual witnessing, with no death thereafter: “They taste no death therein except the first death” [44:56]. Natural death is only a passage from station to station, from a narrow home to a spacious one. The following is recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Do not think death is death:<br>it is life, the utmost of desire.<br>Do not fear death’s assault:<br>it is only passage from here.<br>Put bodily forms off from your selves,<br>and behold the Real in clear direct vision.<\/blockquote><p>I say: the sword that finishes the lower self swiftly is humility and poverty. Whoever humbles himself among his fellows, breaks his own habits and renounces the world finds his lower self dead in the blink of an eye and his spirit alive. He gains the delight of the eye: knowledge of his Master and absence from all else.<\/p><p>The best time for this slaughter is when the lower self is between youth and age. If too young, one fears its return; if too old, its withdrawal may be difficult. It should be complete in qualities through beautiful renunciation and restraint, pleasing to behold through its comely appearance. Likewise, seeing a person of witnessing and vision captivates hearts and carries them swiftly into the Presence of the Knower of unseen things, through his established witnessing of the malakūt. Whoever beholds him forgets ordinary human conditions, is overtaken by spiritual lights, and becomes absent from near and far in remembrance of the Beloved. As the hadith says: “God’s friends are those whose sight brings God to remembrance.”<\/p><p>This lower self must also be unbroken by pursuit of worldly gain and greed, sound and free of blemish, with no created thing holding it in bondage. It then becomes fit for the Presence and delights in witnessing and vision. The adversary of separation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفرق<\/bdi>) no longer contests or disputes with it: the adversary has acknowledged the truth, and the dispute is lifted.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"If a shaykh commands disciples to slaughter their lower selves by breaking customary habits, hesitation over what brings the lower self’s death indicates scant sincerity and a weak final attainment. Haste to overcome it indicates sincerity, success and a sound end. When the lower self dies entirely, the spirit lives through knowledge and perpetual witnessing, with no death thereafter: “They taste no death therein except the first death” [44:56]. Natural death is only a passage from station to station, from a narrow home to a spacious one. The following is recited: Do not think death is death: it is life, the utmost of desire. Do not fear death’s assault: it is only passage from here. Put bodily forms off from your selves, and behold the Real in clear direct vision. I say: the sword that finishes the lower self swiftly is humility and poverty. Whoever humbles himself among his fellows, breaks his own habits and renounces the world finds his lower self dead in the blink of an eye and his spirit alive. He gains the delight of the eye: knowledge of his Master and absence from all else. The best time for this slaughter is when the lower self is between youth and age. If too young, one fears its return; if too old, its withdrawal may be difficult. It should be complete in qualities through beautiful renunciation and restraint, pleasing to behold through its comely appearance. Likewise, seeing a person of witnessing and vision captivates hearts and carries them swiftly into the Presence of the Knower of unseen things, through his established witnessing of the malakūt. Whoever beholds him forgets ordinary human conditions, is overtaken by spiritual lights, and becomes absent from near and far in remembrance of the Beloved. As the hadith says: “God’s friends are those whose sight brings God to remembrance.” This lower self must also be unbroken by pursuit of worldly gain and greed, sound and free of blemish, with no created thing holding it in bondage. It then becomes fit for the Presence and delights in witnessing and vision. The adversary of separation ( الفرق ) no longer contests or disputes with it: the adversary has acknowledged the truth, and the dispute is lifted."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He rebukes the failure of this miracle to affect their hearts, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He rebukes the failure of this miracle to affect their hearts, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C120.1","page":120,"ar":"التدمية الحمراء هي القتيل الذي به جرح أو أثر ضرب أو سم، فإن لم يكن به فهي التدمية البيضاء.","en":"“Red tadmiya” concerns a victim bearing a wound, evidence of a blow, or poison; if none of this is present, it is “white tadmiya.”","anchor":"التدمية الحمراء"}],"english_characters":3759,"arabic_characters":2863},{"id":"q2-74","q":"2:74","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:74 · Hardness of heart and the rivers released by repentance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 121–122","cairo":"I:121–122","source_page_first":121,"source_page_last":122,"ar":"ثُمَّ قَسَتْ قُلُوبُكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ فَهِيَ كَالْحِجارَةِ أَوْ أَشَدُّ قَسْوَةً وَإِنَّ مِنَ الْحِجارَةِ لَما يَتَفَجَّرُ مِنْهُ الْأَنْهارُ وَإِنَّ مِنْها لَما يَشَّقَّقُ فَيَخْرُجُ مِنْهُ الْماءُ وَإِنَّ مِنْها لَما يَهْبِطُ مِنْ خَشْيَةِ اللَّهِ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (74)\nقلت: القسوة والقساوة: هي الصلابة واليبوسة، كالشقوة والشقاوة، يقال حجر قاس، أي: يابس. قال الشاعر:\nوَلاَ أرَى أثراً لِلذِّكرِ في جَسدِي ... والحَبلُ في الجَبَل القاسِي لهُ أثرُ\nو (أو) للإضراب، أو بمعنى الواو، أو للتنويع، فبعضها كالحجارة وبعضها أشد.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ثُمَّ قَسَتْ قُلُوبُكُمْ يا معشر اليهود، ويبست فلم تلن ولم تخشع، مع ما رأت من الآيات كانفجار الحجر بالماء في التيه، وإنزال المنّ والسلوى، وتظليل الغمام، وإحياء الميت وغير ذلك.\nقال الكلبي: (أنكروا بعد ما رأوا ذلك، وقالوا: ما قتلنا، فما كانوا قط أعمى قلباً، ولا أشد تكذيباً منهم لنبيهم عند ذلك) فقلوبهم كالحجارة، بل أشد، أو إن شَبَّهْتم قلوبهم بالحجارة أصبتم، وبما هو أشد أصبتم، بل في الحجارة فضل عليها في اللين، فإن منها ما تتفجر مِنْهُ الْأَنْهارُ الكبار، ومنها ما تشقق فَيَخْرُجُ مِنْهُ العيون الجارية، ومنها ما تهبط من رأس الجبل مِنْ خَشْيَةِ اللَّهِ. وفي بعض الأخبار: «كل حجر تَرَدَّى من رَأسِ جبلٍ فهو من خشية الله» ، وقلوبكم يا معشر اليهود لا تلين ولا تخشع ولا تأتي بخير. نسأل الله السلامة بمنَّه وكرمه.\nالإشارة: كل من أساء الأدب مع أستاذه، أو خرج عن دائرته إلى غيره، قسا قلبه، وذهب حاله ولُبه، فإن رجع قريباً واستدرك ما فات، لان قلبه ونهض حاله، وإلا وقع في مهاوي القطيعة، ولم يأت منه شيء. وللقلب القاسي علامات: منها جمود العين، وطول الأمل، وعدم الحزن على ما فاته من الطاعات وما صدر منه من السيئات، وعدم الفرح بما يصدر منه من الطاعات، فإن المؤمن تسره حسناته وتسيئه سيئاته، ودواؤه: صحبة الفقراء الذاكرين الخاشعين، والجلوس بين يدي العارفين الكاملين، وتعاهد الصيام، والصلاة بالليل والناس نيام، والتضرّع إلى الحي القيوم الذي لا ينام، وللشافعي رضي الله عنه:\nولَمَا قَسَا قَلبي وَضَاقَت مَذَاهِبِي ... جَعَلتُ الرَجَا منِّي لِعَفْوكَ سُلَّمَا\nتَعَاظَمَني ذَنبِي فَلَمَّا قَرَنتُهُ ... بِعفوكَ رَبِّي كَانَ عفوك أَعْظَمَا\nقوله تعالى: وَإِنَّ مِنَ الْحِجارَةِ لَما يَتَفَجَّرُ مِنْهُ الْأَنْهارُ كذلك القلوب القاسية إذا لانت بالإنابة إلى ربها، والرجوع عن مألوفاتها، تتفجر منها أنهار العلوم، وتشقق منها أسرار الحِكَم، ومنها من تذوب من هيبة المتجلي لها، فتندك جبالها، وتزلزل أرض نفوسها، كما قال القائل:\nلَو عَايَنتْ عَينَاكَ يومَ تزلزلتْ ... أَرضْ النفُوس ودُكَّتِ الأجْبَالُ\nلَرأَيتَ شَمسَ الحقِّ يسطعُ نورُها ... حِينَ التزَلْزلِ، والرجَالُ رجالُ\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nثم آيس المؤمنين من الطمع فى إيمان من كان هذا وصفه فقال:","quran_ar":"ثُمَّ قَسَتْ قُلُوبُكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ ذلِكَ فَهِيَ كَالْحِجارَةِ أَوْ أَشَدُّ قَسْوَةً وَإِنَّ مِنَ الْحِجارَةِ لَما يَتَفَجَّرُ مِنْهُ الْأَنْهارُ وَإِنَّ مِنْها لَما يَشَّقَّقُ فَيَخْرُجُ مِنْهُ الْماءُ وَإِنَّ مِنْها لَما يَهْبِطُ مِنْ خَشْيَةِ اللَّهِ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (74)","quran_en":"74. Then your hearts hardened after that, becoming like stones, or harder still. Among stones are some from which rivers burst forth, some that split and release water, and some that fall down in fear of God. God is not unaware of what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Hardness and the senses of “or”","html":"<p>I say: <i>qaswa<\/i> and <i>qasāwa<\/i> mean hardness and dryness, just as <i>shaqwa<\/i> and <i>shaqāwa<\/i> are variant forms of misery. One calls a stone hard, meaning dry. The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">I see no effect of remembrance upon my body,<br>though a rope leaves its mark upon the hard mountain.<\/blockquote><p>“Or” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أو<\/bdi>) marks a transition of emphasis, means “and,” or distinguishes kinds: some hearts are like stones, others harder.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: qaswa and qasāwa mean hardness and dryness, just as shaqwa and shaqāwa are variant forms of misery. One calls a stone hard, meaning dry. The poet says: I see no effect of remembrance upon my body, though a rope leaves its mark upon the hard mountain. “Or” ( أو ) marks a transition of emphasis, means “and,” or distinguishes kinds: some hearts are like stones, others harder."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Stones that answer while hearts remain unmoved","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: then your hearts, Jewish people, hardened and grew dry, neither softening nor humbling themselves, despite the signs they saw: the stone gushing with water in the wandering, manna and quails descending, clouds providing shade, the dead revived, and the rest. Al-Kalbī says: “After seeing this, they denied it and said, ‘We did not kill him.’ Never were their hearts blinder or their denial of their prophet more intense.” Their hearts are like stones—indeed, harder. Or: compare their hearts to stones and you are right; compare them to something harder and you are right. Stones possess a softness surpassing theirs: great rivers burst from some, flowing springs emerge from others, and others fall from mountain-tops in fear of God. A report says: “Every stone that falls from a mountain-top falls through fear of God.” Your hearts, however, neither soften nor humble themselves nor produce good. We ask God for safety through His favour and generosity.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: then your hearts, Jewish people, hardened and grew dry, neither softening nor humbling themselves, despite the signs they saw: the stone gushing with water in the wandering, manna and quails descending, clouds providing shade, the dead revived, and the rest. Al-Kalbī says: “After seeing this, they denied it and said, ‘We did not kill him.’ Never were their hearts blinder or their denial of their prophet more intense.” Their hearts are like stones—indeed, harder. Or: compare their hearts to stones and you are right; compare them to something harder and you are right. Stones possess a softness surpassing theirs: great rivers burst from some, flowing springs emerge from others, and others fall from mountain-tops in fear of God. A report says: “Every stone that falls from a mountain-top falls through fear of God.” Your hearts, however, neither soften nor humble themselves nor produce good. We ask God for safety through His favour and generosity."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Symptoms and treatment of the hardened heart","html":"<p>Whoever behaves discourteously towards his teacher or leaves his circle for another becomes hard-hearted, losing his spiritual state and inner substance. If he returns soon and makes good what was lost, his heart softens and his state rises; otherwise he falls into the depths of severance and nothing comes of him.<\/p><p>Signs of a hard heart include dry eyes, long hopes, no sorrow over missed acts of obedience or committed sins, and no joy in the obedience one performs. A believer is pleased by his good deeds and distressed by his wrongs. Its remedy is companionship with the humble spiritual poor who remember God, sitting before the perfected gnostics, regularly fasting, praying at night while people sleep, and imploring the Living, Self-Subsisting One who never sleeps. Al-Shāfiʿī, God be pleased with him, says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">When my heart hardened and my ways grew narrow,<br>I made my hope a ladder to Your pardon.<br>My sin seemed immense, but when I set it beside<br>Your pardon, Lord, Your pardon was greater.<\/blockquote><p>His words, “Among stones are those from which rivers burst forth,” likewise apply to hardened hearts. When they soften through turning to their Lord and abandoning familiar habits, rivers of knowledge burst forth and mysteries of wisdom open within them. Some melt in awe of the One disclosing Himself, their mountains levelled and the earth of their lower selves shaken. As one says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Had your eyes seen the day the earth of souls was shaken<br>and the mountains levelled,<br>you would have seen the sun of the Real pour out its light<br>amid the shaking, while the men remained men.<\/blockquote><p>And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever behaves discourteously towards his teacher or leaves his circle for another becomes hard-hearted, losing his spiritual state and inner substance. If he returns soon and makes good what was lost, his heart softens and his state rises; otherwise he falls into the depths of severance and nothing comes of him. Signs of a hard heart include dry eyes, long hopes, no sorrow over missed acts of obedience or committed sins, and no joy in the obedience one performs. A believer is pleased by his good deeds and distressed by his wrongs. Its remedy is companionship with the humble spiritual poor who remember God, sitting before the perfected gnostics, regularly fasting, praying at night while people sleep, and imploring the Living, Self-Subsisting One who never sleeps. Al-Shāfiʿī, God be pleased with him, says: When my heart hardened and my ways grew narrow, I made my hope a ladder to Your pardon. My sin seemed immense, but when I set it beside Your pardon, Lord, Your pardon was greater. His words, “Among stones are those from which rivers burst forth,” likewise apply to hardened hearts. When they soften through turning to their Lord and abandoning familiar habits, rivers of knowledge burst forth and mysteries of wisdom open within them. Some melt in awe of the One disclosing Himself, their mountains levelled and the earth of their lower selves shaken. As one says: Had your eyes seen the day the earth of souls was shaken and the mountains levelled, you would have seen the sun of the Real pour out its light amid the shaking, while the men remained men. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then removes the believers’ expectation of faith from those characterized in this way, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then removes the believers’ expectation of faith from those characterized in this way, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3077,"arabic_characters":2487},{"id":"q2-75-77","q":"2:75–77","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:75–77 · Distortion after understanding and the concealment of knowledge","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 122–123","cairo":"I:122–123","source_page_first":122,"source_page_last":123,"ar":"أَفَتَطْمَعُونَ أَنْ يُؤْمِنُوا لَكُمْ وَقَدْ كانَ فَرِيقٌ مِنْهُمْ يَسْمَعُونَ كَلامَ اللَّهِ ثُمَّ يُحَرِّفُونَهُ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما عَقَلُوهُ وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (75) وَإِذا لَقُوا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا قالُوا آمَنَّا وَإِذا خَلا بَعْضُهُمْ إِلى بَعْضٍ قالُوا أَتُحَدِّثُونَهُمْ بِما فَتَحَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْكُمْ لِيُحَاجُّوكُمْ بِهِ عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ أَفَلا تَعْقِلُونَ (76) أَوَلا يَعْلَمُونَ أَنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ ما يُسِرُّونَ وَما يُعْلِنُونَ (77)\nقلت: ضمن الإيمان معنى الإذعان والإقرار ولذلك عداه باللام، وجملة (قد كان) حال من فاعل الإيمان، و (إذا لقوا) عطف على (كان) ، والتقدير: أفتطمعون في إيمانهم والحالة أن مَن سلف منهم كانوا يُحرفون كلام الله، ومن حضر منهم الآن ينافقونكم في دين الله، فلا مطمع في إيمان مَنْ هذا وصفه.\nيقول الحق جلاّ جلاله: أَفَتَطْمَعُونَ يا معشر المسلمين أن يذعن لكم أهل الكتاب ويصدقوكم وَقَدْ كانَ فَرِيقٌ مِنْهُمْ، وهم السبعون الذين ذهبوا مع موسى للاعتذار، يَسْمَعُونَ كَلامَ اللَّهِ حين كلّمهم وكلّفهم بمشاق التوراة، فحرفوا وقالوا: قال افعلوا ما استطعتم، فإذا لم يحصل لهم الإيمان مع سماع الكلام بلا واسطة، فكيف يؤمن لكم هؤلاء، وهم إنما يسمعونه بواسطة الرسالة؟ أو يَسْمَعُونَ كَلامَ اللَّهِ في التوراة ثم يحرفونه، محواً أو تأويلاً، كصفة سيدنا محمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم وآية الرجم وغير ذلك، مِنْ بَعْدِ ما فهموه وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ أنه كلام الله، أو وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ أنهم محرفون ومغيرون لكلام الله.\nوكيف تطمعون أيضاً في إيمانهم وهم منافقون؟ إِذا لَقُوا المؤمنين قالُوا آمَنَّا، وصفة نبيكم مذكورة في كتابنا، وَإِذا خَلا بَعْضُهُمْ إِلى بَعْضٍ لامهم مَن لم ينافق، وقالُوا أَتُحَدِّثُونَهُمْ بِما فَتَحَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْكُمْ من علم التوراة فتطلعونهم عليه لِيُحَاجُّوكُمْ بِهِ أي: يغلبوكم بالحجة عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ في الدنيا والآخرة، فيقولون: كنتم عالمين بنبوة نبينا فجحدتم وعاندتم، أَفَلا تَعْقِلُونَ حتى تطلعوهم على ما فتح الله به عليكم. أو يقول الحق تعالى: أَفَلا تَعْقِلُونَ يا معشر المسلمين فتطمعون في إيمانهم بعد هذه الخصال التي فيهم، قال الحقّ جلّ جلاله:\nأَوَلا يَعْلَمُونَ أَنَّ اللَّهَ لا يخفى عليه شيء، بل يَعْلَمُ ما يُسرونه وما يُعلنونه، فيجازيهم على ما أخفوا وما أعلنوا.\nالإشارة: مَن سبقت له المشيئة بالخذلان، وحكم عليه القدر والقضاء بالحرمان، يرجع إلى الدليل والبرهان، بعد الاستشراف على الشهود والعيان، فيرجع إلى مشاهدة الآثار والرسوم، وينسى ما كان يعهده من دقائق العلوم، سبب ذلك كلَّه: الإخلال بالأدب مع المشايخ والأصحاب، أو مفارقة الإخوان، وعدم مواصلة أهل العرفان، وضم إلى ذلك الإنكار على أولياء الله، وتحريف ما سمعه منهم من مواهب الله، فلا مطمع في رجوعه وإيابه، وقد بَعُد من الفتح وأسبابه، لا سيما إذا اتصف بالنفاق، إذا لقي أهل النسبة أظهر الوفاق، وإذا خلا إلى العامة أظهر الشقاق، فمِثلُ هذا لا يرجى له فلاح، ولا يَسعد بصلاح ونجاح. نعوذ بالله من ذلك.\nولما ذكر الحق تعالى رؤساء اليهود أتبعهم بذكر أتباعهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَفَتَطْمَعُونَ أَنْ يُؤْمِنُوا لَكُمْ وَقَدْ كانَ فَرِيقٌ مِنْهُمْ يَسْمَعُونَ كَلامَ اللَّهِ ثُمَّ يُحَرِّفُونَهُ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما عَقَلُوهُ وَهُمْ يَعْلَمُونَ (75) وَإِذا لَقُوا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا قالُوا آمَنَّا وَإِذا خَلا بَعْضُهُمْ إِلى بَعْضٍ قالُوا أَتُحَدِّثُونَهُمْ بِما فَتَحَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْكُمْ لِيُحَاجُّوكُمْ بِهِ عِنْدَ رَبِّكُمْ أَفَلا تَعْقِلُونَ (76) أَوَلا يَعْلَمُونَ أَنَّ اللَّهَ يَعْلَمُ ما يُسِرُّونَ وَما يُعْلِنُونَ (77)","quran_en":"75. Do you then hope that they will believe you, when a group of them used to hear God’s speech and then distort it after understanding it, knowing what they did? 76. When they meet those who believe, they say, “We believe”; but when they are alone together, they say, “Do you tell them what God has disclosed to you, so that they may dispute with you by it before your Lord? Do you not understand?” 77. Do they not know that God knows what they conceal and what they disclose?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Believing as acknowledgement and submission","html":"<p>I say: believing here includes the sense of submission and acknowledgement; hence it takes the preposition <i>lām<\/i>. “A group of them used to…” is circumstantial relative to the subject of “believe.” “When they meet…” is coordinated with “used to.” The sense is: do you hope for their faith when their predecessors distorted God’s speech and those now present dissemble before you in God’s religion? There is no ground for such hope in one characterized this way.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: believing here includes the sense of submission and acknowledgement; hence it takes the preposition lām . “A group of them used to…” is circumstantial relative to the subject of “believe.” “When they meet…” is coordinated with “used to.” The sense is: do you hope for their faith when their predecessors distorted God’s speech and those now present dissemble before you in God’s religion? There is no ground for such hope in one characterized this way."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Knowing distortion and concealed acknowledgement","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muslims, do you hope that the People of the Book will submit to you and affirm you, when a group of them—the seventy who accompanied Moses to offer an apology—heard God’s speech as He addressed them and imposed the Torah’s arduous requirements, then distorted it, saying, “He said, ‘Do what you can’”? If hearing the speech without an intermediary did not bring them faith, how will these believe you when they hear only through the mediation of a messenger?<\/p><p>Alternatively, they heard God’s speech in the Torah and distorted it by erasure or interpretation, such as the description of our master Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, the stoning verse, and other matters, after understanding it, knowing it was God’s speech—or knowing that they were changing and distorting His speech.<\/p><p>How can you hope for their belief while they dissemble? On meeting believers, they say, “We believe; your prophet’s description is mentioned in our Book.” Alone together, those who did not dissemble blame the others: “Do you tell them what God disclosed to you of the Torah’s knowledge, revealing it so that they may overcome you with an argument before your Lord, in this world and the next? They will say: you knew our prophet’s prophethood but denied it obstinately. Have you no understanding, that you disclose what God has opened to you?” Alternatively, the Real Most High says, “Have you no understanding, Muslims, that you hope for their faith despite these qualities?” The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: do they not know that nothing is hidden from God, that He knows their secret and public acts and recompenses both?<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Muslims, do you hope that the People of the Book will submit to you and affirm you, when a group of them—the seventy who accompanied Moses to offer an apology—heard God’s speech as He addressed them and imposed the Torah’s arduous requirements, then distorted it, saying, “He said, ‘Do what you can’”? If hearing the speech without an intermediary did not bring them faith, how will these believe you when they hear only through the mediation of a messenger? Alternatively, they heard God’s speech in the Torah and distorted it by erasure or interpretation, such as the description of our master Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, the stoning verse, and other matters, after understanding it, knowing it was God’s speech—or knowing that they were changing and distorting His speech. How can you hope for their belief while they dissemble? On meeting believers, they say, “We believe; your prophet’s description is mentioned in our Book.” Alone together, those who did not dissemble blame the others: “Do you tell them what God disclosed to you of the Torah’s knowledge, revealing it so that they may overcome you with an argument before your Lord, in this world and the next? They will say: you knew our prophet’s prophethood but denied it obstinately. Have you no understanding, that you disclose what God has opened to you?” Alternatively, the Real Most High says, “Have you no understanding, Muslims, that you hope for their faith despite these qualities?” The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: do they not know that nothing is hidden from God, that He knows their secret and public acts and recompenses both?"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The danger of repudiating a knowledge once approached","html":"<p>One whom the prior will assigns to abandonment and decree condemns to deprivation returns to evidence and demonstration after approaching witnessing and direct vision. He returns to seeing effects and forms, forgetting the subtle sciences he once knew. The cause is failure in courtesy towards shaykhs and companions, parting from the brethren and ceasing to maintain contact with the people of gnosis, compounded by denial of God’s friends and distortion of the divine gifts heard from them. There is no expectation of his return when he has become so distant from opening and its means—especially if he adds hypocrisy, displaying agreement when meeting people of the spiritual affiliation and discord when alone with the general public. No success or joyful attainment is expected for one like this. We seek refuge in God from it.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"One whom the prior will assigns to abandonment and decree condemns to deprivation returns to evidence and demonstration after approaching witnessing and direct vision. He returns to seeing effects and forms, forgetting the subtle sciences he once knew. The cause is failure in courtesy towards shaykhs and companions, parting from the brethren and ceasing to maintain contact with the people of gnosis, compounded by denial of God’s friends and distortion of the divine gifts heard from them. There is no expectation of his return when he has become so distant from opening and its means—especially if he adds hypocrisy, displaying agreement when meeting people of the spiritual affiliation and discord when alone with the general public. No success or joyful attainment is expected for one like this. We seek refuge in God from it."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having mentioned the Jewish leaders, the Real Most High follows with their followers, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having mentioned the Jewish leaders, the Real Most High follows with their followers, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3050,"arabic_characters":2640},{"id":"q2-78","q":"2:78","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:78 · Uncomprehending imitation and the limits of one’s own knowledge","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 123–124","cairo":"I:123–124","source_page_first":123,"source_page_last":124,"ar":"وَمِنْهُمْ أُمِّيُّونَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ الْكِتابَ إِلاَّ أَمانِيَّ وَإِنْ هُمْ إِلاَّ يَظُنُّونَ (78)\nقلت: أماني: جمع أمنية، وهي في الأصل: ما يُقَدِّرُهُ الإنسان في نفسه من مُنى إذا قدَّر، ولذلك تطلق على الكذب، وعلى ما يتمنى وما يقرأ ، قاله البيضاوي. والاستثناء منقطع، أي: لكن أكاذيب، ويقال: تمنى الرجل، إذا كذب واختلق الحديث، ومنه قول عثمان رضى الله عنه: (والله ما تَمنَّيْتُ ولا تَغَنَّيْتُ منذُ أَسْلَمْتُ) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمِنْهُمْ أي: من اليهود عوام أُمِّيُّونَ لا يقرءون الكتاب ولا يفهمونه، لكن يسمعون من أحبارهم أَمانِيَّ كاذبة، وأشياء يظنونها من الكتاب، ولا علم لهم بصحتها، كتغيير صفته صلّى الله عليه وسلّم\nوغير ذلك، أو مواعيد فارغة، ومطامع خاوية، سمعوها منهم، من أن الجنة لا يدخلها إلا هم، وأن النار لا تمسهم إلا أياماً معدودة، وغير ذلك من أمنيتهم الفارغة وأمانيهم الباطلة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن المنكرين على أهل الخصوصية ثلاث فرق: أهل الرئاسة المتكبرون، والفقهاء المتجمدون، والعوام المقلدون، يصدق عليهم قوله تعالى: وَمِنْهُمْ أُمِّيُّونَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ الْكِتابَ إِلَّا أَمانِيَّ إذ لا علم عندهم يُميزون به المحق من المبطل، وإنما هم مقلدون، فوزرهم على مَن حرمهم بركة الاعتقاد، وأدخلهم في شؤم الانتقاد، ولقد أحسن «ابن البنا» حيث قال في شأن أهل الإنكار:\nواعلمْ رعاكَ اللهُ مِنْ صَديقِ ... أنَّ الورَى حَادُوا عن التحقيق\nإذْ جَهِلُوا النفوسَ والقلوبَ ... وطلبُوا مَا لم يكُنْ مَطلُوبَا\nواشْتَغَلُوا بعالم الأبدان ... فالكلّ ناء منْهمُ ودَانِ\nوأنكَرُوا مَا جَهِلوا وزَعموا ... أنْ لَيسَ بعدَ الجسمِ شَيْءٌ يُعلمُ\nوكفَّرُوا وزندقُوا وبدَّعُوا ... إذَا دَعَاهُم اللَّبِيبُ الأوْرَعُ\nكلٍّ يرى أنْ لَيسَ فوقَ فَهْمِهِ ... فَهْمٌ ولاَ عِلْمٌ وراءَ عِلْمِهِ\nمُحْتَجِباً عَنْ رؤيةِ المَراتبْ ... عَلّ يُسْمَى عَالماً وَطالبِ\nهَيْهَاتَ هذا كُلَّه تقْصِيرُ ... يأنَفُهُ الحَاذقُ والنّحرير\nثم توعّد أهل التحريف من الأحبار، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمِنْهُمْ أُمِّيُّونَ لا يَعْلَمُونَ الْكِتابَ إِلاَّ أَمانِيَّ وَإِنْ هُمْ إِلاَّ يَظُنُّونَ (78)","quran_en":"78. Among them are unlettered people who know the Book only through notions; they do no more than conjecture.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The range of amāniyy","html":"<p>I say: <i>amāniyy<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أمانيّ<\/bdi>) is the plural of <i>umniyya<\/i>. Originally it means what a person forms within himself, from <i>manā<\/i>, “to determine or conceive.” It is therefore applied to falsehood, what one wishes for, and what one recites. Al-Bayḍāwī says this. The exception is disconnected: “but [they know] falsehoods.” One says a man <i>tamannā<\/i> when he lies and invents a report. Thus ʿUthmān, God be pleased with him, said: “By God, since embracing Islam I have neither fabricated tales nor sung.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: amāniyy ( أمانيّ ) is the plural of umniyya . Originally it means what a person forms within himself, from manā , “to determine or conceive.” It is therefore applied to falsehood, what one wishes for, and what one recites. Al-Bayḍāwī says this. The exception is disconnected: “but [they know] falsehoods.” One says a man tamannā when he lies and invents a report. Thus ʿUthmān, God be pleased with him, said: “By God, since embracing Islam I have neither fabricated tales nor sung.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Unexamined notions about Scripture","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: among the Jews are unlettered common people who neither read nor understand the Book. They hear false notions from their learned men, supposing things to belong to Scripture without knowing their truth—for example, the alteration of the Prophet’s description,<\/p><p>God bless him and grant him peace, and other matters. Or they hear empty promises and groundless hopes: that none but they will enter Paradise, that the Fire will touch them for only a few days, and their other empty wishes and false expectations. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: among the Jews are unlettered common people who neither read nor understand the Book. They hear false notions from their learned men, supposing things to belong to Scripture without knowing their truth—for example, the alteration of the Prophet’s description, God bless him and grant him peace, and other matters. Or they hear empty promises and groundless hopes: that none but they will enter Paradise, that the Fire will touch them for only a few days, and their other empty wishes and false expectations. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Mistaking the limits of one’s understanding for the limits of reality","html":"<p>Those who deny the people of special election are of three kinds: arrogant people of leadership, rigid jurists and imitative commoners. His words apply to them: “Among them are unlettered people who know the Book only through notions,” since they lack knowledge to distinguish the person in truth from the person in error and merely imitate. Their burden falls upon those who deprive them of the blessing of trusting acknowledgement and lead them into the ill fortune of hostile criticism. Ibn al-Bannāʾ expresses the condition of deniers well:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Know, my friend—may God protect you—<br>that people have turned away from realization.<br>They knew not souls and hearts,<br>and sought what was not to be sought.<br>They occupied themselves with the world of bodies,<br>all of them, near and far.<br>They denied what they did not know, claiming<br>that nothing beyond the body could be known.<br>They accused him of unbelief, heresy and innovation<br>when the intelligent, scrupulous man invited them.<br>Each thinks that no understanding exceeds his own,<br>and no knowledge lies beyond his knowledge,<br>veiled from seeing the degrees,<br>perhaps called a scholar or a seeker.<br>Far from it! All this is shortcoming<br>which the skilful and discerning reject.<\/blockquote>","authorial":true,"text":"Those who deny the people of special election are of three kinds: arrogant people of leadership, rigid jurists and imitative commoners. His words apply to them: “Among them are unlettered people who know the Book only through notions,” since they lack knowledge to distinguish the person in truth from the person in error and merely imitate. Their burden falls upon those who deprive them of the blessing of trusting acknowledgement and lead them into the ill fortune of hostile criticism. Ibn al-Bannāʾ expresses the condition of deniers well: Know, my friend—may God protect you— that people have turned away from realization. They knew not souls and hearts, and sought what was not to be sought. They occupied themselves with the world of bodies, all of them, near and far. They denied what they did not know, claiming that nothing beyond the body could be known. They accused him of unbelief, heresy and innovation when the intelligent, scrupulous man invited them. Each thinks that no understanding exceeds his own, and no knowledge lies beyond his knowledge, veiled from seeing the degrees, perhaps called a scholar or a seeker. Far from it! All this is shortcoming which the skilful and discerning reject."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then threatens the learned men who distort, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then threatens the learned men who distort, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C123.1","page":123,"ar":"لأن القارئ يقدر أن كلمة كذا بعد كذا.","en":"Because the reader reckons that such-and-such a word follows such-and-such another.","anchor":"وما يقرأ"}],"english_characters":2333,"arabic_characters":1743},{"id":"q2-79","q":"2:79","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:79 · Writing falsehood for worldly gain","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 124–125","cairo":"I:124–125","source_page_first":124,"source_page_last":125,"ar":"فَوَيْلٌ لِلَّذِينَ يَكْتُبُونَ الْكِتابَ بِأَيْدِيهِمْ ثُمَّ يَقُولُونَ هَذَا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ لِيَشْتَرُوا بِهِ ثَمَناً قَلِيلاً فَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا كَتَبَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا يَكْسِبُونَ (79)\nقلت: (ويل) : كلمة يستعملها كل واقع في هلكة، وأصلها العذاب والهلكة، وهو في الأصل مصدر لا فعل له، وسوغ الابتداء به الدعاء، وقال أبو سعيد الخدري عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم: «الويلُ وادٍ في جَهنَّم» [لو سيرت فيه جبال الدنيا لانماعت] .\nيقول الحق جلاّ جلاله: فَوَيْلٌ لِلَّذِينَ يَكْتُبُونَ الْكِتابَ بِأَيْدِيهِمْ تحريفاً لكتاب الله، ويَقُولُونَ هَذَا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ خوفاً من أن تزول رئاستهم، وينقطع عنهم ما كانوا يأخذونه من سفلتهم، نزلت في أحبار اليهود لما قدم النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم المدينة، خافوا أن تزول رئاستهم، فاحتالوا في تعويق اليهود عن الإسلام، وكانت صفة النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم فى التوراة: «حسن الوجه، حسن الشعر، أكحل العينين، ربعة» ، فغيروها، وكتبوا: طوالاً، أزرق، سبط الشعر، فَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا كَتَبَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا يَكْسِبُونَ، ويأخذون من سفلتهم، فهو وإن كان كثيرا فى الحس فهو، بالنسبة إلى ما استوجبوه من العذاب الأليم، قليل.\nالإشارة: ينزجر بهذه الآية صنفان: أحدهما: علماء الأحكام، إذا أفتوا بغير المشهور، رغبة فيما يقبضون على الفتوى من الحطام الفاني، وكذلك القضاة إذا حكموا بالهوى، رغبة فيما يقبضون من الرشا، أو يحصلونه من الجاه، فَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا كَتَبَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا يَكْسِبُونَ الثاني: أهل الرئاسة والجاه من أولاد الصالحين وغيرهم، فإنهم إذا رأوا أحداً قام بولاية أو نسبة خافوا على زوال رئاستهم، فيحتالون على الناس بالتعويق عن الدخول في طريقته، فيكتبون في ذلك سفسطات وترهات، يُنفِّرون الناس عن اتباع الحق، وَيَأْبَى اللَّهُ إِلَّا أَنْ يُتِمَّ نُورَهُ وَلَوْ كَرِهَ الْكافِرُونَ.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى بعض أمانيهم الفارغة، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَوَيْلٌ لِلَّذِينَ يَكْتُبُونَ الْكِتابَ بِأَيْدِيهِمْ ثُمَّ يَقُولُونَ هَذَا مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ لِيَشْتَرُوا بِهِ ثَمَناً قَلِيلاً فَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا كَتَبَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَوَيْلٌ لَهُمْ مِمَّا يَكْسِبُونَ (79)","quran_en":"79. Woe to those who write the Book with their own hands and then say, “This is from God,” to obtain a paltry price for it. Woe to them for what their hands have written, and woe to them for what they earn.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The cry of woe","html":"<p>I say: <i>wayl<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ويل<\/bdi>) is an expression used by someone falling into destruction. Its basic sense is punishment and ruin. Originally it is a verbal noun without a corresponding verb; its supplicatory force permits it to begin a nominal sentence. Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī relates from the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace: “Wayl is a valley in Hell.” [Were the mountains of this world made to travel through it, they would melt.]<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-58\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B054]<\/a><\/p><p class=\"translator-note-ref\">[Translator’s print-source notes: <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/R07-01\">[R07-01]<\/a>]<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: wayl ( ويل ) is an expression used by someone falling into destruction. Its basic sense is punishment and ruin. Originally it is a verbal noun without a corresponding verb; its supplicatory force permits it to begin a nominal sentence. Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī relates from the Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace: “Wayl is a valley in Hell.” [Were the mountains of this world made to travel through it, they would melt.] [B054] [Translator’s print-source notes: [R07-01] ]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Distortion to preserve authority","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: woe to those who write the Book with their hands, distorting God’s Book, and say, “This is from God,” fearing the loss of their leadership and the payments they receive from their lower ranks. The verse was revealed concerning the Jewish learned men: when the Prophet came to Medina, they feared losing their authority and devised means to prevent Jews from embracing Islam. His description in the Torah was: beautiful in face and hair, dark-rimmed of eye, of medium stature. They changed it to: tall, blue-eyed and straight-haired. Woe for what their hands wrote and what they earn and take from their lower ranks. However great it appears to the senses, it is little compared with the painful punishment it incurs.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: woe to those who write the Book with their hands, distorting God’s Book, and say, “This is from God,” fearing the loss of their leadership and the payments they receive from their lower ranks. The verse was revealed concerning the Jewish learned men: when the Prophet came to Medina, they feared losing their authority and devised means to prevent Jews from embracing Islam. His description in the Torah was: beautiful in face and hair, dark-rimmed of eye, of medium stature. They changed it to: tall, blue-eyed and straight-haired. Woe for what their hands wrote and what they earn and take from their lower ranks. However great it appears to the senses, it is little compared with the painful punishment it incurs."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Corrupt rulings and inherited spiritual authority","html":"<p>Two groups should be restrained by this verse. First are scholars of legal judgements who issue opinions contrary to the established position (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشهور<\/bdi>) out of desire for the perishable gain received for their fatwās; likewise judges who decide according to desire, seeking bribes or status. Woe to them for what their hands write and what they earn.<\/p><p>Second are people of leadership and rank, including descendants of the righteous. When they see someone established in friendship with God or a spiritual affiliation, they fear losing their authority and plot to prevent people entering his path. They write sophistries and nonsense that repel people from following truth. “But God will only perfect His light, though the unbelievers dislike it” [9:32].<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Two groups should be restrained by this verse. First are scholars of legal judgements who issue opinions contrary to the established position ( المشهور ) out of desire for the perishable gain received for their fatwās; likewise judges who decide according to desire, seeking bribes or status. Woe to them for what their hands write and what they earn. Second are people of leadership and rank, including descendants of the righteous. When they see someone established in friendship with God or a spiritual affiliation, they fear losing their authority and plot to prevent people entering his path. They write sophistries and nonsense that repel people from following truth. “But God will only perfect His light, though the unbelievers dislike it” [9:32]."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High mentions some of their empty wishes, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High mentions some of their empty wishes, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C124.1","page":124,"ar":"انماعت: أي ذابت. قلت: والعبارة التي بين المعكوفتين ليست من الحديث المذكور، بل هي من كلام عطاء بن يسار، كما في تفسير الطبري والواحدي، أو من كلام أبي سعيد الخدري، كما في تفسير البغوي.","en":"Inmāʿat means “melted.” I say: the words between square brackets do not form part of the cited prophetic report. They are instead the words of ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, as in the commentaries of al-Ṭabarī and al-Wāḥidī, or of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, as in al-Baghawī’s commentary.","anchor":"لو سيرت فيه جبال الدنيا لانماعت"}],"english_characters":2056,"arabic_characters":1719},{"id":"q2-80-82","q":"2:80–82","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:80–82 · False assurance and the conditions of saintly intercession","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 125–127","cairo":"I:125–127","source_page_first":125,"source_page_last":127,"ar":"وَقالُوا لَنْ تَمَسَّنَا النَّارُ إِلاَّ أَيَّاماً مَعْدُودَةً قُلْ أَتَّخَذْتُمْ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ عَهْداً فَلَنْ يُخْلِفَ اللَّهُ عَهْدَهُ أَمْ تَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ مَا لا تَعْلَمُونَ (80) بَلى مَنْ كَسَبَ سَيِّئَةً وَأَحاطَتْ بِهِ خَطِيئَتُهُ فَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (81) وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ أُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ الْجَنَّةِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (82)\nقلت: (بلى) : حرف جواب كنعم، والفرق بينهما أن (بلى) لا يقع إلا في جواب النفي ويصير إثباتاً، تقول: ألم يأت زيد؟ فتقول بلى. أي: أتى، ومثله: قالُوا لَنْ تَمَسَّنَا النَّارُ فقال تعالى: (بلى) أي تمسكم، بخلاف نعم فإنها لتقرير ما قبلها نفياً أو إثباتاً، فإذا قيل: ألم يأت زيد؟ فقلت: نعم، أي لم يأت، وإذا قيل: هل أتى زيد فقلت:\nنعم، أي أتى. وقد نظم ذلك بعضهم فقال:\n«نعَمْ» لتقرير الذي قبلها ... إثباتاً أو نفياً، كذا قرَّرُوا\n«بلى» جواب النفي لكنه ... يصير إثباتاً، كذا حرَّرُوا\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَقالُوا أي: بنو إسرائيل في أمانيهم الباطلة: لَنْ تَمَسَّنَا النَّارُ إِلَّا أَيَّاماً مَعْدُودَةً أربعين يوماً مقدار عبادة العجل، ثم يخلفنا فيها المسلمون. قال الحق جل جلاله: قُلْ لهم يا محمد: أَتَّخَذْتُمْ بذلك عهداً عند الله فَلَنْ يُخْلِفَ اللَّهُ عَهْدَهُ أَمْ تَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ مَا لا تَعْلَمُونَ- بَلى تمسكم النار وتخلدون فيها لأن مَنْ كَسَبَ سَيِّئَةً أي: كفراً ومات عليه، وَأَحاطَتْ بِهِ خَطِيئَتُهُ أي: أحدقت به، واستولت عليه، فَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ- وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بما نُزِّل على محمد صلّى الله عليه وسلّم وَعَمِلُوا بشريعته المطهرة الأعمال الصَّالِحاتِ أُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ الْجَنَّةِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ هذه عادته تعالى إذا ذكر فريقاً شفع بضده ترغيباً وترهيباً وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن كثيرا من الناس يعتمدون على صحبة الأولياء، ويُطلقون عنان أنفسهم في المعاصي والشهوات، ويقولون: سمعنا من سيدي فلان يقول: مَن رآنا لا تمسه النار. وهذا غلط وغرور، وقد قال- عليه الصلاة والسّلام- لابنته: «يا فاطمةَ بنتَ مُحمَّد، لا أُغْنِي عَنْكِ مِنَ اللهِ شيئاً، اشترِيِ نفسك من الله» . وقال للذي قال: ادع الله أن أكون رفيقك في الجنة فقال له: «أعِنِّي عَلَى نَفْسِكَ بكَثْرةِ السُّجُود» . نعم، هذه المقالة: إن صدرت من ولي متمكن مع الله فهي حق، لكن بشرط العمل ممن رآه بالمأمورات وترك المحرمات، فإن المأمول من فضل الله، ببركة أوليائه، أن يتقبل الله منه أحسن ما عمل، ويتجاوز عن سيئاته، فإن الأولياء المتمكنين اتخذوا عند الله عهداً فلن يخلف الله عهده وهو أن من تعلق بهم وتمسك بالشريعة شفعوا فيه.\nوالغالب على مَن صَحِبَ أولياء الله المتمكنين- الحفظ وعدم الإصرار، فمن كان كذلك لا تمسه النار، وفي الحديث: «إذا أحب الله عَبْداً لَمْ يضرُّه ذَنْب» ، يعني: يُلهم التوبة سريعاً، كما قيل لأهل بدر: «افعلوا ما شئتم فقد غَفَرتْ لَكُم» . ولا يتخذ عند الله العهد إلا أهل الفناء والبقاء، لأنهم بالله فيما يقولون، فليس لهم عن أنفسهم إخبار، ولا مع غير الله قرار، وأما من لم يبلغ هذا المقام فلا عهد له لأنه بنفسه، فمن تعلّق بمثل هذا فهو على خطر، وبالله التوفيق.\nقوله تعالى: بَلى مَنْ كَسَبَ سَيِّئَةً، من اقتنى حب الدنيا أحاطت به أشغالها وعلائقها، فهو في نار القطيعة مقيم، أحاط به سرادق الهموم والأكدار، تلدغه عقارب الشكوك والأغيار، بخلاف من أشرقت عليه أنوار الإيمان، وصحب أهل الشهود والعيان، فإنه في رَوْح وريحان وجنة ورضوان، متعنا الله بذلك فى الدارين. آمين.\nثم قرّعهم على نقض العهد الذي أُخذ عليهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالُوا لَنْ تَمَسَّنَا النَّارُ إِلاَّ أَيَّاماً مَعْدُودَةً قُلْ أَتَّخَذْتُمْ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ عَهْداً فَلَنْ يُخْلِفَ اللَّهُ عَهْدَهُ أَمْ تَقُولُونَ عَلَى اللَّهِ مَا لا تَعْلَمُونَ (80) بَلى مَنْ كَسَبَ سَيِّئَةً وَأَحاطَتْ بِهِ خَطِيئَتُهُ فَأُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ النَّارِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (81) وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ أُولئِكَ أَصْحابُ الْجَنَّةِ هُمْ فِيها خالِدُونَ (82)","quran_en":"80. They say, “The Fire will touch us only for a few numbered days.” Say, “Have you taken a covenant with God—God will not break His covenant—or do you say of God what you do not know?” 81. Rather, whoever earns evil and is encompassed by his offence—such are the companions of the Fire, abiding therein. 82. But those who believe and do righteous deeds are the companions of the Garden, abiding therein.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Balā and naʿam","html":"<p>I say: <i>balā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بلى<\/bdi>) is a response particle like <i>naʿam<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نعم<\/bdi>), but the two differ. <i>Balā<\/i> answers a negative and turns it into an affirmation. If asked, “Did Zayd not come?” one replies <i>balā<\/i>: he did come. Likewise, they said, “The Fire will not touch us,” and God replied <i>balā<\/i>: it will touch you. <i>Naʿam<\/i>, however, confirms the preceding statement, negative or affirmative. Asked “Did Zayd not come?” <i>naʿam<\/i> confirms that he did not. Asked “Did Zayd come?” it confirms that he came. Someone has put this into verse:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\"><i>Naʿam<\/i> confirms what goes before,<br>affirmation or negation: so they teach.<br><i>Balā<\/i> replies to a negative,<br>but makes it affirmative: so they explain.<\/blockquote>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: balā ( بلى ) is a response particle like naʿam ( نعم ), but the two differ. Balā answers a negative and turns it into an affirmation. If asked, “Did Zayd not come?” one replies balā : he did come. Likewise, they said, “The Fire will not touch us,” and God replied balā : it will touch you. Naʿam , however, confirms the preceding statement, negative or affirmative. Asked “Did Zayd not come?” naʿam confirms that he did not. Asked “Did Zayd come?” it confirms that he came. Someone has put this into verse: Naʿam confirms what goes before, affirmation or negation: so they teach. Balā replies to a negative, but makes it affirmative: so they explain."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The offence that encompasses its possessor","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Children of Israel utter their false wish that the Fire will touch them for only a few numbered days—forty, the period of calf-worship—after which the Muslims will replace them there. The Real tells Muḥammad to ask: have you taken a covenant from God to that effect, which He will not break, or do you speak about Him without knowledge? Rather, the Fire will touch you and you will remain there, for whoever acquires an evil deed—unbelief—and dies upon it, his offence surrounding and mastering him, belongs among the companions of the Fire, abiding therein.<\/p><p>Those who believe what was revealed to Muḥammad and perform righteous works according to his purified Law are the companions of Paradise, abiding therein. It is His customary manner, when mentioning one group, to follow it with its opposite, for encouragement and warning. Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Children of Israel utter their false wish that the Fire will touch them for only a few numbered days—forty, the period of calf-worship—after which the Muslims will replace them there. The Real tells Muḥammad to ask: have you taken a covenant from God to that effect, which He will not break, or do you speak about Him without knowledge? Rather, the Fire will touch you and you will remain there, for whoever acquires an evil deed—unbelief—and dies upon it, his offence surrounding and mastering him, belongs among the companions of the Fire, abiding therein. Those who believe what was revealed to Muḥammad and perform righteous works according to his purified Law are the companions of Paradise, abiding therein. It is His customary manner, when mentioning one group, to follow it with its opposite, for encouragement and warning. Through God is success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Companionship is not permission to disobey","html":"<p>Know that many rely upon companionship with God’s friends while giving their souls free rein in disobedience and desires. They say, “We heard our master so-and-so say: whoever sees us is not touched by the Fire.” This is error and delusion. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, told his daughter: “Fāṭima daughter of Muḥammad, I cannot avail you against God: purchase your own soul from God.” To the man who asked, “Pray that I may be your companion in Paradise,” he said, “Help me against your own soul through abundant prostration.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-59\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B055]<\/a><\/p><p>Certainly, if this saying comes from a friend of God firmly established with Him, it is true—but on condition that the person who sees him obeys the commands and abandons forbidden things. What may be hoped from God’s bounty, through His friends’ blessing, is that He accepts the best of that person’s deeds and overlooks his wrongs. The firmly established friends have taken a covenant with God, which He will not break: they intercede for someone who attaches himself to them while adhering to the Sacred Law.<\/p><p>The usual condition of one who accompanies firmly established friends of God is protection and non-persistence in sin. Such a person is not touched by the Fire. In the hadith: “When God loves a servant, sin does not harm him”—meaning that He inspires swift repentance—as the people of Badr were told, “Do what you wish: I have forgiven you.” Only the people of effacement and subsistence take this covenant, because they are through God in what they say. They report nothing from their own selves and find no abiding place with other-than-God. Someone who has not reached this station has no such covenant, because he remains through himself. One attached to such a person is in danger. Through God is success.<\/p><p>His words, “Rather, whoever earns evil…”: whoever acquires love of the world is surrounded by its occupations and attachments. He remains in the fire of severance, encompassed by the enclosure of cares and impurities, stung by the scorpions of doubts and other-than-God. By contrast, one upon whom faith’s lights shine and who accompanies the people of witnessing and direct vision enjoys repose, fragrance, a garden and good pleasure. May God give us that enjoyment in both abodes. Amen.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that many rely upon companionship with God’s friends while giving their souls free rein in disobedience and desires. They say, “We heard our master so-and-so say: whoever sees us is not touched by the Fire.” This is error and delusion. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, told his daughter: “Fāṭima daughter of Muḥammad, I cannot avail you against God: purchase your own soul from God.” To the man who asked, “Pray that I may be your companion in Paradise,” he said, “Help me against your own soul through abundant prostration.” [B055] Certainly, if this saying comes from a friend of God firmly established with Him, it is true—but on condition that the person who sees him obeys the commands and abandons forbidden things. What may be hoped from God’s bounty, through His friends’ blessing, is that He accepts the best of that person’s deeds and overlooks his wrongs. The firmly established friends have taken a covenant with God, which He will not break: they intercede for someone who attaches himself to them while adhering to the Sacred Law. The usual condition of one who accompanies firmly established friends of God is protection and non-persistence in sin. Such a person is not touched by the Fire. In the hadith: “When God loves a servant, sin does not harm him”—meaning that He inspires swift repentance—as the people of Badr were told, “Do what you wish: I have forgiven you.” Only the people of effacement and subsistence take this covenant, because they are through God in what they say. They report nothing from their own selves and find no abiding place with other-than-God. Someone who has not reached this station has no such covenant, because he remains through himself. One attached to such a person is in danger. Through God is success. His words, “Rather, whoever earns evil…”: whoever acquires love of the world is surrounded by its occupations and attachments. He remains in the fire of severance, encompassed by the enclosure of cares and impurities, stung by the scorpions of doubts and other-than-God. By contrast, one upon whom faith’s lights shine and who accompanies the people of witnessing and direct vision enjoys repose, fragrance, a garden and good pleasure. May God give us that enjoyment in both abodes. Amen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He rebukes their breach of the covenant taken from them, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He rebukes their breach of the covenant taken from them, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3883,"arabic_characters":3117}];
